WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:18.180
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies and&nbsp;
germs, this is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.980
episode of The Tim Ferriss Show. I'm going to&nbsp;
skip my preamble to get to the guest today, Niall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:31.320
Ferguson. You can find him on Twitter @nfergus,&nbsp;
F-E-R-G-U-S. MA, DPhil, FRSE—we may get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.360
what that is—is the Millbank Family Senior Fellow&nbsp;
at the Hoover Institution, Stanford University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.580
and a senior faculty fellow of the Belfer Center&nbsp;
for Science and International Affairs at Harvard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.960
He's the author of 16 books, good Lord, that's&nbsp;
a lot of books, including The Pity of War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:53.040
The House of Rothschild, Empire, Civilization,&nbsp;
and Kissinger, 1923-1968: The Idealist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.440
which won the Council on Foreign Relations Arthur&nbsp;
Ross Prize. He's an award-winning filmmaker too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.840
having won an international Emmy for his PBS&nbsp;
series The Ascent of Money. His 2018 book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.860
The Square and the Tower, was a New York Times&nbsp;
bestseller and also adapted for television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.860
by PBS as Niall Ferguson’s Networld.
In 2020, he joined Bloomberg Opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.020
as a columnist. In addition, he is the founder&nbsp;
and managing director of Greenmantle LLC, a New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.240
York based advisory firm, a co-founder of Ualá,&nbsp;
a Latin American financial technology company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.340
and a trustee of The New York Historical Society,&nbsp;
the London Based Center for Policy Studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.020
and the newly founded University of&nbsp;
Austin. His latest book, Doom, subtitled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.700
The Politics of Catastrophe, was published&nbsp;
last year by Penguin and was shortlisted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.184
the Lionel Gerber Prize. Niall, so nice&nbsp;
to see you. Thanks for making the time.&nbsp;

00:01:43.184 --> 00:01:46.860
Niall Ferguson: Good to be with you, Tim.&nbsp;
And guilty as charged, all that you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.600
said is true and I cannot deny it.
Tim Ferriss: All of the things and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.880
we are going to unpack all sorts of bits and&nbsp;
pieces of that. And I wanted to start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.560
an observation, and you can correct this&nbsp;
observation if it's an illusion, a mirage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.164
a misreading of the facts. Noah Feldman&nbsp;
says hello, by the way, as a side note and —&nbsp;

00:02:09.164 --> 00:02:12.120
Niall Ferguson: Hello back to Noah.
Tim Ferriss: And that is, he put into words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.420
something that has struck me about your career and&nbsp;
that is that it's not really one career. You have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:24.240
at least three careers, so a major historian, so&nbsp;
the best of the best, played the ivory tower game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:31.800
brilliantly well, then public historian/public&nbsp;
intellectual, we could parse out or relabel any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:37.200
number of those. And then afterwards, you have&nbsp;
advisory firms and the company involvement and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.440
so on. The first question I want to ask related to&nbsp;
this, and certainly you can revise that, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:51.840
was your first experience in reaching a broader&nbsp;
public with your ideas, outside of academia?&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:03:01.200
Niall Ferguson: I always felt that if I was going&nbsp;
to be a historian, it should not be for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:11.700
tiny exclusive audience confined to ivory towers,&nbsp;
whether in Oxford or Cambridge or Harvard. My hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:20.760
when I was a schoolboy was a British historian,&nbsp;
A.J.P. Taylor, who not only wrote terrific books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.160
I think the first history book I remember&nbsp;
engaging with was his illustrated history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.680
of the First World War. But he also would write&nbsp;
newspaper columns and do lectures on television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.820
and get into public rows. And that always&nbsp;
struck me as a highly attractive way of life.&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:46.260
You would be in the morning sitting in your&nbsp;
oak paneled study, surrounded by books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.960
smoking your pipe and giving tutorials. But in&nbsp;
the afternoon, you'd bash out 1,000 words for The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.940
Daily Express, and then you'd deliver a quick&nbsp;
lecture on the 1848 Revolutions for the BBC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:04.980
and then you'd be in time for a high table at&nbsp;
seven. That was always the plan. I never aspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:13.560
to be a dry as dust academic at all. In fact,&nbsp;
it's striking how successful I've executed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:20.040
plan that I hatched when I was a teenager.
Tim Ferriss: This leads into, I suppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:26.520
the question maybe even part answered it,&nbsp;
which is, let me set the table by saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.920
I have a lot of involvement with friends&nbsp;
in academia, with researchers who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:39.360
de facto involved in academic settings, and&nbsp;
there's a lot of fear-based career planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:48.780
in those circles, sort of seeking a prestige and&nbsp;
social support or endorsement. You seem to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:55.200
been a skeptic of whatever container people&nbsp;
have attempted to put you within and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:03.300
become an iconoclast in many respects. Is that&nbsp;
lack of fear, what appears to be a lack of fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.400
something that was developed? Is it simply&nbsp;
because you had already hatched a plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.500
leave the confines, for instance, in the one case&nbsp;
of academia, therefore you weren't worried about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:24.840
them booting you out. Where does that openness&nbsp;
to criticizing what's around you come from?&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:30.647
Niall Ferguson: Well, you have to remember&nbsp;
that I'm old, I'm 58. When my career —&nbsp;

00:05:30.647 --> 00:05:34.380
Tim Ferriss: You've been at it for a while though.
Niall Ferguson: Yeah, but the launch zone in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:42.060
mid 1980s when I was an undergraduate at Oxford,&nbsp;
was very different compared with the launch zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.880
at a Harvard or a Yale or a Stanford today. And&nbsp;
I'm not sure it's possible to be me today. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:55.740
fact, it almost certainly isn't. We forget that&nbsp;
the 1980s were a high point of academic freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:01.320
because academic freedom's not something that's&nbsp;
always been there, it had to kind of be fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:08.040
for, in the later 20th Century. And I was one&nbsp;
of the beneficiaries of a tremendous climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:13.800
of intellectual freedom in Britain in the '80s.&nbsp;
The battles had really been fought in the '60s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:20.280
and '70s or earlier. And we took for granted&nbsp;
that we could say the most outrageous things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:29.640
calculatedly offensive things, and pay no price.&nbsp;
I used to call myself a punktory, because I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:36.840
been a schoolboy when the Sex Pistols burst onto&nbsp;
the scene in 1976. Three years later, Margaret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:45.060
Thatcher became Prime Minister and she was almost&nbsp;
as infuriating to many people as Johnny Rotten.&nbsp;

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:50.940
And I loved that. I just loved the fact&nbsp;
the Sex Pistols shook up music and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:56.160
shook up politics. And I arrived at Oxford&nbsp;
in the early '80s at the same time as my old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.120
friend Andrew Sullivan. And we were just&nbsp;
odious. We were just the most obnoxious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:08.700
the most obnoxious young men. And we took pride&nbsp;
in our outrageous discourse, as it would be called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:13.080
today. There was a famous occasion when somebody&nbsp;
threw a, well it wasn't famous, it was famous to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:18.300
us, somebody threw a party to celebrate the&nbsp;
deployment of cruise and Pershing missiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:25.260
to Western Europe. And the invitation was the&nbsp;
neck of a champagne bottle with a mushroom cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:32.340
coming out of it. And that was one of the milder&nbsp;
things that we did. At that time, there were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:40.140
limited downside risks to being obnoxious&nbsp;
and I think that was true in the US too.&nbsp;

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:46.380
I said to Peter Thiel the other day, "You couldn't&nbsp;
be you now. 18-year-old Peter Thiel now would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.600
be kind of destroyed on the launchpad, whereas&nbsp;
you could be as obnoxious as we were at Oxford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:58.080
at Stanford, and actually positively benefit&nbsp;
from it." There were some risks to going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:04.020
into the academic profession even then though,&nbsp;
because there's a period in your academic career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.920
even in the 1980s, when you're capable&nbsp;
of being destroyed on the launchpad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:17.340
because you do need people to write letters of&nbsp;
reference and you do need committees to give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:23.340
jobs and you need them also to agree to publish&nbsp;
your first book, all that stuff. And so I'll give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:29.100
you an example of my risk averse behavior. I&nbsp;
did a lot of journalism in the '80s when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:35.340
a graduate student. I couldn't have afforded to&nbsp;
do a PhD otherwise because I really had no money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:41.100
beyond a very meager scholarship.
And I was doing research in Germany, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.660
was quite expensive in those days for a British&nbsp;
student. And so I did a lot of journalism, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:51.480
did it under assumed names. There was a chap named&nbsp;
Alec Campbell. There was F.F. Gillespie who wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:57.720
for Punch magazine. And for several years, I led a&nbsp;
double life, where Niall Ferguson was the somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:06.600
right-leaning, but terribly rigorous scholar,&nbsp;
but these other personas would be writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:12.180
even for the Daily Mail, which was a really&nbsp;
scandalous thing for an aspiring academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:17.520
to do. And I would say, I can remember&nbsp;
quite early on, an old rival of mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:24.720
going around telling everybody that in fact, Alec&nbsp;
Campbell was me and those wire why pieces in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.604
Daily Mail were by me, and I probably —
Tim Ferriss: Pause for a second —&nbsp;

00:09:28.604 --> 00:09:30.780
Niall Ferguson: He thought that&nbsp;
would kill me and it didn't.&nbsp;

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:34.974
Tim Ferriss: What was the blood feud between&nbsp;
you and this arch rival? What was he hoping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.974 --> 00:09:39.600
gain? Was he hoping to gain from this or just&nbsp;
simply annihilate you by outing this scandal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:45.000
of identity flak jackets that you've been using?
Niall Ferguson: Well, Maurice Cowling, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:52.740
great conservative historians of Cambridge, once&nbsp;
characterized the three virtues in life as irony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:59.940
geniality, and malice and I think my contemporary&nbsp;
was motivated by all three. It wasn't that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:06.780
meditating career extinction, it was just a&nbsp;
combination of irony, geniality, and malice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:11.940
He presumably was sitting at high table and there&nbsp;
was a lull of the conversation and he said, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:17.700
by the way, did you know that that awful Alec&nbsp;
Campbell in the Daily Mail is really Niall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:24.780
Ferguson?" And I do remember being seized by panic&nbsp;
when I realized that he'd outed me. But by that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:29.040
point, I think I'd got far enough up the ladder —
Tim Ferriss: You've reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.320
escape velocity on some level.
Niall Ferguson: Just about, or maybe there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.920
another theory about this, which I'll briefly&nbsp;
share. In those days, you've got to remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:41.340
that before the internet, communications&nbsp;
were really quite terrible in the horizontal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:48.780
domain. You had vertical communication, you could&nbsp;
listen to the BBC if they didn't ban your single,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:55.260
you could do that. But Oxford and Cambridge didn't&nbsp;
communicate very much. And so you could be outed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.420
in Oxford but still get a job in Cambridge&nbsp;
because the news have not in fact traveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:07.560
across the country in the same way that my mentor,&nbsp;
Norman Stone got a job in Oxford, despite having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:13.860
behaved quite outrageously in Cambridge. But his&nbsp;
Cambridge backers were really trying to export&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.540
him to Oxford, so they perjured themselves in&nbsp;
their letters of reference. And no one in Oxford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:23.460
really bothered to pick up a phone and say, "Is&nbsp;
any of this true?" I think what really happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:28.140
was that I was outed in Oxford, but in the&nbsp;
nick of time, got a job in Cambridge and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:32.760
able to start a new life with a clean sheet.
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to make a confession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:37.980
as a knuckle-dragging Long Islander.&nbsp;
I don't actually know what high table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:43.860
is or the high table. I can imagine in my&nbsp;
mind some British high society function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:51.720
maybe with some fantastic adult beverages. But&nbsp;
what image should high table conjure in my mind?&nbsp;

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.860
Niall Ferguson: Well, you're right&nbsp;
about the beverages. You've watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.364
some Harry Potter movies.
Tim Ferriss: I have, yes.&nbsp;

00:11:58.364 --> 00:12:04.620
Niall Ferguson: That's all you need to know.&nbsp;
It's basically the case that Hogwarts is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:11.820
sort of entertaining amalgam of Eaton and an&nbsp;
Oxford College, quite a lot of it's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:17.580
shot at Christ Church. And you'll remember&nbsp;
that when they have their spectacular feasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:26.336
the teachers sit on a slightly elevated dais&nbsp;
with a big long table. And not Gandalf, wrong —&nbsp;

00:12:26.336 --> 00:12:31.067
Tim Ferriss: It does look like whiskers.
Niall Ferguson: I get Dumble —&nbsp;

00:12:31.067 --> 00:12:31.680
Tim Ferriss: Dumbledore.
Niall Ferguson: Dumbledore, I get my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.680
children's fiction confused because I have to&nbsp;
watch so much of it. Dumbledore gives his speeches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:43.080
from that high table. And if you go to dinner at&nbsp;
Christ Church, essentially you will see a version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:48.120
of Hogwarts, slightly less visually striking,&nbsp;
but the same basic idea. And the thing that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:53.839
attractive about Ox and Cambridge, if you come&nbsp;
from Glasgow, remember, you think Long Island is—&nbsp;

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:55.560
Tim Ferriss: A backwater?
Niall Ferguson: —low?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.560 --> 00:13:01.200
Let me tell you about Glasgow.
I came from what was once one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:07.620
great industrial metropoles of the British Empire,&nbsp;
but by the 1970s was Rust Belt. We were doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:14.100
Hillbilly Elegy before J.D. Vance was even born, I&nbsp;
think. And it was quite a messy place. And Oxford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:22.200
by comparison, just looked like a really high-end&nbsp;
BBC costume drama, in which the men wore tweed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:29.160
suits and they dined at high table. And at high&nbsp;
table the most delicious wines were served.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.180
And all of this was irresistibly attractive&nbsp;
to me when I was a teenager. I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:37.800
"I'm going to get out of Glasgow and I'm going&nbsp;
to have a study like that and I'm going to eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:42.300
at high table and wear a tweed suit,"&nbsp;
and I did those things and it was great.&nbsp;

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.707
Tim Ferriss: Did it live up to the dreams?
Niall Ferguson: Yes.&nbsp;

00:13:45.707 --> 00:13:49.020
Tim Ferriss: What did you like about it?
Niall Ferguson: In those days, when I made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:55.020
it first to high table, it was in Cambridge&nbsp;
and I was at Christ's College that had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.020 --> 00:14:04.980
immortalized in a novel, The Masters, by C.P. Snow&nbsp;
in which Cambridge politics provides the drama and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:12.720
academic politics has its own peculiar appeal as&nbsp;
Henry Kissinger famously said, "It's so poisonous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:17.580
because the stakes are so low," very good line.
Tim Ferriss: Now you are actually of good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:22.500
authority to say, did Kissinger also say, "I left&nbsp;
academics because I couldn't stand the politics,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:27.480
something along those lines. Or is that just one&nbsp;
of those attributed to Abraham Lincoln or Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.860
Wilde quotes floating around on the internet?
Niall Ferguson: Oh no, Kissinger has said so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:37.980
many things that you deny he said something and&nbsp;
the next day you find it in one of the documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:46.140
so he probably said that, but it certainly fits.&nbsp;
But I got to Christ's and there were in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:53.220
three masters, two former masters and one current&nbsp;
serving master, regularly dining at high table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.220 --> 00:15:00.480
And the vicious way in which the former masters&nbsp;
undermined the serving master was my introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:06.840
to academic politics. Luckily one of the former&nbsp;
masters was Sir John Plumb, Jack Plumb, a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:13.200
influential historian of that generation. He had&nbsp;
come from humble origins in the town of Leicester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:18.840
but had risen through the academic ranks, had&nbsp;
been involved at Bletchley Park during the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:24.300
had got to know the Rothschild family and&nbsp;
had been introduced to the delights of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:31.307
Château Lafite by the Rothschilds and he used&nbsp;
to serve Château Lafite at special occasions —&nbsp;

00:15:31.307 --> 00:15:35.173
Tim Ferriss: This is the nice wine I assume?
Niall Ferguson: It's one of the great Bordeaux.&nbsp;

00:15:35.173 --> 00:15:38.220
Tim Ferriss: Oh my goodness, of course.
Niall Ferguson: Not available in Long Island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:47.280
but I'm in Glasgow, but available in Cambridge.&nbsp;
And so I fell in with him and he would on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:57.480
85th birthday, serve some fantastic vintage. And&nbsp;
the younger dons, the engineering fellows would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:03.480
sort of take a couple of sips and then leave most&nbsp;
of it undrunk and go back to the laboratories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:10.260
And this would send Jack into a rage, he would&nbsp;
seethe. He was a very cantankerous old man. And I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:14.880
as the junior-most fellow, I remember on one&nbsp;
occasion I was such a shameless sycophant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:19.740
but also with a taste for Château Lafite. I said&nbsp;
after they'd all gone, I said, "Jack, it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:26.220
terrible shame to waste this wine. I'm going to&nbsp;
drink the dregs from the glasses that they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:32.220
left." And he loved me for that. And I also lived&nbsp;
to tell the tale. None of them had any obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:37.560
lethal infectious diseases. And it's the most&nbsp;
wonderful wine. And once you've drunk Lafite, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:41.520
hard to go back to what you were drinking before.
Tim Ferriss: You're spoiled, spoiled for life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:46.500
That scene should make it into a screenplay&nbsp;
at the turn of some act, that is a fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:48.647
scene. "May I drink the dregs?"
Niall Ferguson: It was a good —&nbsp;

00:16:48.647 --> 00:16:53.624
Tim Ferriss: "Would it please you&nbsp;
if I were to drink the dregs?"&nbsp;

00:16:53.624 --> 00:16:57.480
Niall Ferguson: It was a good move because Jack&nbsp;
Plumb was one of the great academic patrons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:03.480
Simon Schama was one of Jack's proteges and&nbsp;
Linda Colley. And so by sucking up to Jack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:07.680
I did my career no harm at all. Because&nbsp;
when Jack wrote a reference, when he wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:12.420
a letter of reference, he would begin, "Not&nbsp;
since Edward Gibbon has a historian written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:20.220
at this level of quality." Wildly hyperbolic&nbsp;
references. Yeah, academic politics involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:28.620
that kind of odious behavior. And I'm afraid&nbsp;
I was capable of it in my twenties. Still am.&nbsp;

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:34.980
Tim Ferriss: Skilled, one might say. Let's go&nbsp;
back to A.J.P. Taylor. Number of questions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:40.440
A.J.P. Taylor. I had never heard this name, I'm&nbsp;
ashamed to admit, until doing some research for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:48.900
this conversation. And number of questions again&nbsp;
with the baseline of Long Island kept in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:53.040
because these are probably going to be&nbsp;
fairly one-on-one questions. One is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.940 --> 00:18:01.320
how should people think about defining philosophy&nbsp;
of history? That's number one. And number two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:10.740
is the Taylor line related to historical&nbsp;
sensibility being similar to musical sensibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:16.380
and if you could just say more about that.
Niall Ferguson: The philosophy of history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:23.700
is barely studied or indeed taught. And yet you&nbsp;
can't really be an historian without a philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:28.500
of history. You have to understand the nature of&nbsp;
causation. These days, nobody bothers with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:34.560
which is why a lot of academic history is garbage.&nbsp;
I became fascinated by the philosophy of history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:41.100
at that time when I was drinking Château Lafite&nbsp;
with Jack Plumb and realized that there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:46.200
very central problem, namely that any causal&nbsp;
statement, this is obvious to philosophers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:52.440
and it's obvious to people who do the law,&nbsp;
implies a counterfactual. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:59.700
if we say that the conversation between Tim&nbsp;
Ferriss and Niall Ferguson in November 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:05.400
led to the outbreak of war between China&nbsp;
and the United States the following year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:08.340
that implies that if we&nbsp;
hadn't had the conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:12.120
the war wouldn't have happened. And so the&nbsp;
what-if question, "Well, what if they hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:17.400
had the conversation?" is a legitimate question.
In the same way, if you think that Hitler was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:23.580
key architect of World War II and it wouldn't have&nbsp;
happened without him, then it's legitimate to ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:28.380
"What would've happened if Hitler had in&nbsp;
fact been assassinated prior to September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:35.700
1939?" Those what-if questions were of great&nbsp;
importance to me as a young scholar and teacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:40.620
that was how I used to teach students. I would&nbsp;
say, "Well what if that hadn't happened? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:48.240
if Britain hadn't intervened in 1914?" Taylor was&nbsp;
a historian who was sympathetic to that approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:55.740
His view was a somewhat skeptical, ironical view&nbsp;
that men only learn from history how to make new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.740 --> 00:20:04.320
mistakes. And his books are shot through with the&nbsp;
role of contingency. One of the things that made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:11.160
Taylor attractive to me in parentheses, was his&nbsp;
brilliant pro style up there with George Orwell as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:19.080
one of the master stylists of the 20th Century.
And a tremendous master of paradox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:28.080
and of one-liners too. The line about the&nbsp;
1848 revolutions as being the turning point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:34.380
when Germany failed to turn. All that stuff I&nbsp;
found exciting because being able to write like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:39.600
that is also the way to convince a reader,&nbsp;
to be able to talk like that is to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:45.660
an audience, so that's how the philosophy&nbsp;
of history relates to the style of history.&nbsp;

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:54.960
The second question that you asked about music,&nbsp;
his assertion that the historical sensibility is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.960 --> 00:21:01.440
a bit like the receptiveness to music is right.&nbsp;
History's not a science. It can't be a science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:07.860
because we can't rerun events in laboratory and&nbsp;
see if, consistently, war breaks out in 1939 with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:14.700
or without Hitler. So what we're doing when we&nbsp;
studied the past as a great Oxford philosopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:19.380
of history, R.G. Collingwood said, is, "We're&nbsp;
kind of reconstituting past human experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:26.340
from the bits and pieces that have been left&nbsp;
behind. And as we do that, it's a very subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:34.200
process of mental reenactment of experience."
And I'm not sure that Taylor was wrong to compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:43.020
it to that musical side of the human psyche.&nbsp;
Interestingly, as someone else said much the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:48.300
thing, a man named Fritz Kraemer, who was Henry&nbsp;
Kissinger's great mentor during and after World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:55.380
War II and Kraemer said that Kissinger had this&nbsp;
kind of sensibility. He was attuned to history. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.380 --> 00:22:04.680
think that's very, very right. You've got to have&nbsp;
that the ear for it. And it's quite obvious when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:10.440
one reads a book when a historian's tone deaf and&nbsp;
shouldn't really have gone into the business, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:15.180
as it would be obvious if they tried to conduct&nbsp;
an orchestra or play a concerto. Yeah, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:21.240
it's much closer to music than it is to science.
Tim Ferriss: How does that show up for you having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:29.940
a trained ear in historical resonance, let's just&nbsp;
say, the ear for history. How does that show up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:38.880
when it is part of an author's constitution&nbsp;
or developed repertoire? What does it look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:43.440
like in their writing or their thinking?
Niall Ferguson: When I was writing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:48.300
history of the Rothschild banks&nbsp;
and family back in the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.800 --> 00:23:00.060
I spent a lot of my time reading through dusty old&nbsp;
letters and diary entries and minutes of meetings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:06.000
And the most striking documents were the&nbsp;
letters that the Rothschild brothers wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.780
to one another. There were five of them.&nbsp;
They'd come out of the Frankfurt ghetto.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:17.220
They had built, within an amazingly short time,&nbsp;
the most powerful financial institution in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:23.880
already by the 1820s. And they wrote almost&nbsp;
daily to one another because they no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:28.860
lived together. One was in London, one was in&nbsp;
Paris, one was in Vienna, one was in Naples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:34.380
and one was in Frankfurt. And so these&nbsp;
letters were almost more like phone calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:40.080
and they were extremely hard to decipher&nbsp;
because they wrote in Hebrew characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:47.880
which were extremely difficult to read, even if&nbsp;
you knew Hebrew. The only way I could access the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:52.800
letters was to have a scholar who could read&nbsp;
them, a wonderful man named Mordechai Zucker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:57.420
read them aloud onto audio cassettes.
And so I spent a lot of time listening to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.420 --> 00:24:03.960
these letters. And when he read them aloud, they&nbsp;
were mostly German with occasional Hebrew words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:12.000
They were electrifying documents. They cast&nbsp;
an entirely new light on the 19th Century and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:18.000
they were entirely absorbing, not least&nbsp;
because they were so regular, so it was very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:24.120
high frequency correspondence. And I can remember&nbsp;
one letter, and I'll never forget the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:30.840
of hearing it, in which the eldest of the brothers&nbsp;
whose name was Amschel, described what it was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:39.060
for the first time to own land. He bought a garden&nbsp;
on the outskirts of Frankfurt. Prior to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:47.700
Jews had not been allowed to own real estate.&nbsp;
And he, as a result of the upheavals caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:52.800
by the French Revolution, suddenly was able to&nbsp;
do that. And he writes to the brothers saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:58.920
"I bought the garden. And last night I&nbsp;
slept in it. I slept outside in the garden."&nbsp;

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:08.160
And he described the feeling, and I remember, and&nbsp;
it still makes my spine tingle, the feeling of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:17.340
excitement that this quite dry man — because he&nbsp;
was almost had the mentality of the accountant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:22.860
amongst the brothers — but this quite dry&nbsp;
man had done that. He'd slept outdoors for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:30.060
the first time in a garden that he owned. And I&nbsp;
was moved to tears and I thought, "What is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:36.540
like? Why is this such a moving document?" And I&nbsp;
remembered the moment in Beethoven's "Fidelio,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:43.020
when the prisoners are let out into the open&nbsp;
air, he actually uses the expression “freier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:49.560
Luft”—”free air”—and that prisoner's chorus,&nbsp;
when they sing what it is to be in the open air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:57.360
struck me as exactly the appropriate music&nbsp;
for that moment. And I remember putting it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:07.500
my then stereo player and listening to Beethoven&nbsp;
and listening again to the letter and thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:12.960
"This is an enormously important document&nbsp;
which captures what emancipation means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:17.820
what it is to be suddenly given a civil&nbsp;
right that you've previously been denied."&nbsp;

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:26.880
And often in my career, I've had these epiphanies,&nbsp;
moments when a document suddenly reveals something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:37.260
profound and often that moment of connection has a&nbsp;
musical dimension to it. The best historians have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:44.400
that ability to bring the experiences of the&nbsp;
dead alive, bring them back to life, make you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:52.080
empathize with them despite the distance in time&nbsp;
and in space and in experience. And that's a hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:59.220
thing to do. And not many people can do it well.&nbsp;
Indeed, many historians these days don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:04.020
try because they don't feel that our mission is&nbsp;
to reconstitute past experience. They feel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:10.380
our mission is to go back and judge people in&nbsp;
the past for their sins by our standards. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:16.080
that means that there's a project which I think&nbsp;
is very hostile to historical understanding, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:21.660
change the mission of the historian, instead&nbsp;
of trying to reconstitute past experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:27.240
we are really just sitting there, passing&nbsp;
judgments on past actions. I'm very against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:31.860
that. I think it produces very bad history.&nbsp;
It also produces quite boring history.&nbsp;

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:38.700
Tim Ferriss: If we go back to&nbsp;
A.J.P., what did A.J.P. stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:41.616
for? Actually, I should know this, but —
Niall Ferguson: AlanTaylor, I forget —&nbsp;

00:27:41.616 --> 00:27:43.140
Tim Ferriss: Alan.
Niall Ferguson: — probably John Percivale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:47.100
but I might be wrong. But he was known as&nbsp;
Alan to his intimates. But in those days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:52.500
people quite often published with only their&nbsp;
initials, created perhaps a certain distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:59.400
between the author and the public. I never&nbsp;
contemplated being N.C. Ferguson, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.780
It's just too off-putting, isn't it?
Tim Ferriss: Non-Commissioned Officer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:06.300
Ferguson. I do like it, though. Dr. Taylor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:12.960
said, as you mentioned, mentally learned from&nbsp;
history how to make new mistakes. Now he seems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:19.380
I don't know this person at all, but as you&nbsp;
describe, being a master of paradox, also being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:25.920
a wordsmith and gifted in that particular way,&nbsp;
that there may be some truth in this statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:34.800
but he also dedicated his life to history. If you&nbsp;
got a few drinks, a few glasses of Château Lafite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:39.480
into Dr. Taylor and said, "All right, look, I&nbsp;
know that's a great line, but why is history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:44.280
important to you? Why have you dedicated your life&nbsp;
to this?" How do you think he might answer that?&nbsp;

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:47.940
Niall Ferguson: I'm pretty sure he was Mr.&nbsp;
Taylor, in those days, people didn't bother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:48.785
getting doctorates.
Tim Ferriss: Just —&nbsp;

00:28:48.785 --> 00:28:50.340
Niall Ferguson: They were regarded as —
Tim Ferriss: I'm using "doctor" more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:53.760
in the Dr. Seuss capacity.
Niall Ferguson: The English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.760 --> 00:29:00.240
regarded doctorates as a German invention&nbsp;
that one didn't need to sully one's life with.&nbsp;

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:02.220
Tim Ferriss: Mr. Taylor.
Niall Ferguson: Perhaps wisely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:15.960
Taylor's conviction was that most people thought&nbsp;
wrongly about the past, had misconceptions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:23.760
it, and these misconceptions led them into&nbsp;
error. One of the most controversial books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:30.360
he ever wrote was a book on the origins of&nbsp;
the Second World War, in which he argued very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:38.280
paradoxically as a contrarian that really Hitler&nbsp;
had not been a particularly unusual German leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:43.560
and that Hitler's foreign policy had in some&nbsp;
ways been the continuation of previous foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:48.960
policies, and that the war was really the fault&nbsp;
of the blundering incompetents in London and Paris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:56.280
who had made the policy of appeasement and had&nbsp;
pursued it very ineptly. The book is still worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.280 --> 00:30:00.840
reading even although these days, most people&nbsp;
would disagree with Taylor because they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:07.380
argue, as I would argue, that Hitler was a kind&nbsp;
of monster whose ideology and whose personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:14.400
were very unusual and a departure from German&nbsp;
tradition. At any event, the book is brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:22.080
and it's designed to force you to reassess your&nbsp;
assumptions about why World War II happened.&nbsp;

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:30.600
I'm motivated by the same belief that most people&nbsp;
think wrong things about the past and base their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:37.500
actions on these erroneous assumptions. And&nbsp;
the job of the historian is to challenge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:42.300
conventional wisdom about past events in&nbsp;
the belief that the conventional wisdom's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:49.920
actually quite a dangerous thing. So it's to me&nbsp;
uninteresting to write a book that essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:55.500
repeats the arguments that have been made before.&nbsp;
I have no motivation to write a book unless it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.500 --> 00:31:01.560
challenging what people have thought about the&nbsp;
past, whether it's the origins of the First World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:06.840
War, which I wrote a book about, whether it's the&nbsp;
nature of 20th century conflict, whether it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:13.380
history of money. Whatever it is, I'm motivated&nbsp;
to say something that challenges received opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:21.240
in the belief that by doing that, I might possibly&nbsp;
improve decision-making in the present and future.&nbsp;

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:26.100
I believe, and I've always believed, that we&nbsp;
are trying to learn lessons from the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:29.820
And when Taylor said that men only learn&nbsp;
from the past how to make new mistakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:34.500
he wasn't dismissing the possibility of learning&nbsp;
from history, but he was being ironical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:41.040
It's probably right that no matter how hard we&nbsp;
try, we'll end up making new mistakes. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:47.700
not a counsel of despair. And I passionately&nbsp;
believe that the study of history must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:53.520
motivated by a desire to do better or at least&nbsp;
to fail better next time. So that I think is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:59.100
you'd have got from Taylor if you'd plied him with&nbsp;
Château Lafite, although Taylor would've resisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:09.780
your very American impulse to get a sincere&nbsp;
answer. And I am enough of an American now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:14.520
to give sincere answers, but the typical&nbsp;
British dawn of Taylor's generation would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:18.780
just — he would not have been able to&nbsp;
resist the temptation to tease you.&nbsp;

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:24.210
Tim Ferriss: Maybe I need more of that. Maybe&nbsp;
I should spend more time at the high table.&nbsp;

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:26.940
Niall Ferguson: I can arrange that.
Tim Ferriss: You have given me an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:31.440
appetite for some tweed, I will be honest.
Niall Ferguson: It's good in winter. The thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:37.980
about tweed is it's there for places where there's&nbsp;
no central heating. And these Oxford colleges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:44.220
their buildings often date back to before&nbsp;
the Reformation. It wasn't as if England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:48.540
was famous for its central heating, even in&nbsp;
the 20th century. And so to understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:52.740
tweed suit as a phenomenon, you have to&nbsp;
remember these studies are pretty cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.740 --> 00:32:57.660
even if you've got a fire in the grate. Now that&nbsp;
we are heading towards another energy crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:04.080
I believe the tweed suit will recover, maybe not&nbsp;
in the United States, but in England. Pretty soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:07.620
there's going to be a run on tweed suits.
Tim Ferriss: You heard it here first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:11.196
folks. Alpha, Niall Ferguson is long tweed.
Niall Ferguson: Long tweed.&nbsp;

00:33:11.196 --> 00:33:15.060
Tim Ferriss: So a few just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:20.520
miscellanea. Mordechai, incredible name. Always&nbsp;
one of my favorites. You mentioned Percivale. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:25.920
may not have been the P of the A.J.P., but also&nbsp;
a fantastic name. Could you say a bit more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:35.220
the role of contingency, I guess the throughline,&nbsp;
one of the throughlines, of Taylor's writing? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:40.860
you can answer these out of order, of course.&nbsp;
For someone who wanted to get a good taste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:46.800
of his writing because your description of&nbsp;
paradox and the use of paradox and powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:52.860
metaphor and prose is really appealing to&nbsp;
me. Where would you suggest someone start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.860 --> 00:33:58.020
or where would you suggest perhaps I start?
Niall Ferguson: Well, I'll give you a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.020 --> 00:34:08.880
of examples of historical contingency.&nbsp;
First, the decision for war in 1914&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:18.840
was taken in Britain on a weekend, which was an&nbsp;
unusual time for the British cabinet to meet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:27.300
And when they went into the meeting, I think&nbsp;
it's fair to say that nobody really knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:34.140
how it would turn out. In particular, the then&nbsp;
Chancellor of the Exchequer, David Lloyd George,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.140 --> 00:34:38.040
was expected to be against intervention&nbsp;
because he had been a man of the left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:46.200
and it was not obvious that intervening in the&nbsp;
war was a particularly left liberal thing to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:52.200
There were plenty of people in the cabinet who had&nbsp;
deep reservations about intervening in the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.200 --> 00:34:58.200
and it was seen as something that the&nbsp;
conservators were much more enthusiastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:05.520
for. And so people expected Lloyd George to&nbsp;
speak up against intervention. He'd already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:10.920
given indications prior to August second that&nbsp;
he had doubts about the wisdom of action.&nbsp;

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:17.940
But he didn't. Even although he was handed a note&nbsp;
by one of his cabinet colleagues encouraging him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:23.220
to speak up, he had decided that it would&nbsp;
ultimately be better for David Lloyd George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:27.060
if Britain went to war. And he was right because&nbsp;
he ended up becoming Prime Minister during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:34.020
war because Herbert Asquith had descended&nbsp;
into a kind of alcoholic fog. That's a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:41.580
example of how one individual's action or lack&nbsp;
of action can have very profound consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:47.400
And I wrote a lot about that in The Pity Of&nbsp;
War and in the book Virtual History because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:54.900
I think it's important for people to understand&nbsp;
that while there are great historical forces at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.900 --> 00:36:02.520
work, there were powerful forces driving Europe&nbsp;
towards conflict prior to 1914, there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:08.880
very important role for contingency, and that&nbsp;
continues to be true today. And once you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:12.587
about the historical process — and in a similar&nbsp;
way, let me give you a contemporary example.&nbsp;

00:36:12.587 --> 00:36:16.560
Tim Ferriss: May I pause just for one sec? So in&nbsp;
terms of contingency, just the use of that word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:20.940
I think of contingency in the context&nbsp;
of say contingency plan. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:27.960
do you mean it's a split test with an&nbsp;
alternate version of historical reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:33.480
where someone did or did not do something, did&nbsp;
or did not play a part in a given conversation?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:37.620
Is that what you mean by contingency?
Niall Ferguson: Contingency here means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:43.560
a relatively small event or decision.&nbsp;
And it doesn't need to be a decision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:51.120
It can be something accidental, has very major&nbsp;
consequences. And historical causations like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:58.620
something relatively small, can have tremendous&nbsp;
ramifications. I'll give you another illustration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:06.960
This year, most people, including the US&nbsp;
government, thought that if Russia invaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:13.560
Ukraine, the Ukrainian government would quite&nbsp;
quickly fold, and it was assumed that Volodymyr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:20.400
Zelenskyy, the Ukrainian president, would bail. He&nbsp;
didn't. He gave his famous response, "I don't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:30.660
an air ticket. I want ammunition." And Zelenskyy's&nbsp;
courage when they were closing in on Kyiv with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:39.000
high probability that they would assassinate,&nbsp;
the Russians turned the course of history in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:45.180
way that I think is now quite widely understood.
People know Zelenskyy is an important historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:51.660
figure. He gets a lot of attention because he is&nbsp;
a charismatic figure who understands how to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:56.820
modern media to communicate with an audience.&nbsp;
That's the benefit of having a very seasoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.820 --> 00:38:02.700
entertainer as your president. But I think what's&nbsp;
really important there is that his courage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:09.180
particularly the famous video selfie video where&nbsp;
he says, "I'm here. The defense minister is here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:12.300
We're here." They're standing in the streets of&nbsp;
Kyiv. The Russians are closing in at that point on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:21.300
the capital. That was a tremendous act of courage.&nbsp;
But it emboldened ordinary Ukrainians not to fold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:26.700
and it also intimidated the collaborators who&nbsp;
were ready to help the Russians, not to act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:34.200
So the contingency there is if Zelenskyy had gone&nbsp;
according to our expectation and taken the plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:41.280
then Putin would've had Kyiv within a matter&nbsp;
of days or weeks, and the war would be over.&nbsp;

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:48.780
So I think one of the things that's exciting about&nbsp;
the study of history is you are trying to remind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:54.360
yourself again and again that what happened,&nbsp;
that what we know happened, might have gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:58.860
the other way. That the Cuban Missile Crisis&nbsp;
ended in both sides essentially backing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:04.920
was not predetermined. There was a moment when a&nbsp;
Soviet submarine commander gave the order to fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:12.840
a nuclear torpedo at US naval surface ships. So&nbsp;
we came within a hair's breadth of World War III.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:18.960
These alternate worlds, these histories&nbsp;
that didn't happen, have to be alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:23.340
in your mind when you are writing history.
The fatal mistake is to write history as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:27.720
it was bound to happen the way it happened.&nbsp;
And this, of course, is the mistake that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:34.500
great majority of historians make. Forgetting&nbsp;
that, we don't know at the time, at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:43.200
we didn't know the morning of the 24th of February&nbsp;
that Zelenskyy would stand his ground. Nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:48.360
knew that. I wonder if even Zelenskyy at that&nbsp;
moment knew what it was that he was going to do.&nbsp;

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:54.180
So I say all this because I think it's really&nbsp;
important to convey to your listeners and viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.180 --> 00:40:00.600
how exciting history is and how studying it makes&nbsp;
you understand the course of events in your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:07.860
life better removes that passivity that sometimes&nbsp;
people succumb to. If you think great historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:12.660
forces are going to have inevitable outcomes, if&nbsp;
you have a deterministic view of the historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:19.200
process, it's very easy to lapse into fatalism.&nbsp;
There's the other trap, which is the conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:23.160
theories. "Well, the truth of the matter is&nbsp;
that actually, Soros and the Rothschilds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:28.620
orchestrating all this." Again, you throw&nbsp;
up your hands and you abandon the attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:35.520
to understand how the historical process works.&nbsp;
Okay. What should one read to get a flavor of Alan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:40.020
Taylor's extraordinary talent? He developed —
Tim Ferriss: You have a very good short-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:42.042
memory, by the way. You have&nbsp;
a very good short-term memory.&nbsp;

00:40:42.042 --> 00:40:43.740
Niall Ferguson: I do my best.
Tim Ferriss: Please continue.&nbsp;

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:48.360
Niall Ferguson: Taylor's masterpiece is quite&nbsp;
a dense read, and I wouldn't recommend it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:52.980
The Struggle for Mastery in Europe. That's the&nbsp;
book I most admire, but it's probably the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:56.444
thing to start with. It's —
Tim Ferriss: The widowmaker.&nbsp;

00:40:56.444 --> 00:41:01.500
Niall Ferguson: Yeah. I mean, I love the book,&nbsp;
but I'm a little idiosyncratic. I like long and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:10.080
dense books. But the essays that he wrote,&nbsp;
which were often book reviews, are available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.080 --> 00:41:16.140
in paperback collections. In the old days, they&nbsp;
used to publish the essays of historians. I wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:21.780
they still did because I would've many more books&nbsp;
to my credit if I could just repackage my essays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:26.700
as books. But his books of essays are really&nbsp;
worth reading. In the same way that if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:32.700
to get to Orwell, don't just read Animal Farm&nbsp;
and 1984. Read Orwell's amazing essays. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:37.980
are four fantastic volumes of Orwell's essays.&nbsp;
And he and Taylor between them were just such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:45.180
masters of short form English prose. You will&nbsp;
just feel like you are eating Turkish delight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:49.860
as you read your way through their essays.
Tim Ferriss: Just in the vein of adding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:54.540
your tally of books published, if the&nbsp;
book doesn't exist with the title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.540 --> 00:42:00.540
The Histories that Didn't Happen, just saying,&nbsp;
has a nice ring to it, a bit of alliteration, easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:05.040
to grasp, not too abstract. In any case, I just —
Niall Ferguson: Lewis Namier was a great Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:12.360
historian who said that the key to history was&nbsp;
having a sense of what didn't happen. And I always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:12.360 --> 00:42:21.840
think of Thelonious Monk's line about jazz. "It's&nbsp;
the notes you don't play." And as a jazz fan, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:27.480
think history has to have that kind of Thelonious&nbsp;
Monk feel to it where you're telling the reader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:34.500
"This didn't happen, but it nearly did, and people&nbsp;
at the time thought about it." This is the key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:42.480
rule, by the way. You can't just fantasize and&nbsp;
devise counterfactuals that are entirely out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.480 --> 00:42:46.800
your imagination. You have to go with things that&nbsp;
people at the time thought might happen. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:51.600
a really important guardrail on counterfactual&nbsp;
history. The fancy term for what-if history or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:55.980
histories that didn't happen is counterfactual&nbsp;
history. And I'm a passionate believer in it.&nbsp;

00:42:55.980 --> 00:43:02.580
And I'm proud to say that despite my making the&nbsp;
argument for counterfactual history back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:09.360
1990s, that's a long time ago now, it's still&nbsp;
counterculture. It's still what the cool kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:15.180
do in history, and the establishment still looks&nbsp;
down on it. I used to be annoyed about that. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:19.440
used to think, "Why don't they understand? Why&nbsp;
do these people not see you have to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:26.700
what didn't happen?" And now I'm glad that it's&nbsp;
still cool and minority and underground. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.700 --> 00:43:32.460
fine with me. You established historians carry&nbsp;
on doing what you are doing. You carry on saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:37.440
"We are only here to study what did happen." You&nbsp;
carry on doing that. But just know that the cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:43.560
kids have seen through this and they know that&nbsp;
it's really BS. You have to talk about what didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:49.620
happen to understand what did happen, obviously.
Tim Ferriss: Or all the things that so nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:55.380
didn't happen. And I'm no historian. I make&nbsp;
no claims otherwise. Don't play one on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:55.380 --> 00:44:00.840
internet. However, I did listen. Don't know how&nbsp;
you feel about this podcast, but I have enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:05.460
many episodes of Hardcore History by Dan Carlin,&nbsp;
who would not call himself a historian either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:05.460 --> 00:44:09.960
but he did an episode on the assassination&nbsp;
of Franz Ferdinand and everything that came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:17.640
of that. And when you dig into the specifics, I&nbsp;
mean, it is just mind-boggling to think how that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:26.580
came about and how many near-misses.&nbsp;
It just boggles the mind to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:33.780
how as you put, if you zoom too far out and just&nbsp;
view history as these inevitable tectonic plates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:40.260
then there's a fatalism that can set in and you&nbsp;
throw up your hands and move on to just staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:44.400
at TikTok all day or whatever you might do. And&nbsp;
at the same time, if you bleed into the conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:49.484
theory side of things, same outcome. But I need&nbsp;
to do more reading. Part of what you describe —&nbsp;

00:44:49.484 --> 00:44:53.520
Niall Ferguson: By the way, Dan Carlin is&nbsp;
great. Dan Carlin is great. I love what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.520 --> 00:45:00.480
does. I haven't heard that particular episode, but&nbsp;
anything that gets a wider audience for history is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:08.400
good. And I recently heard his great interview&nbsp;
with Elon Musk about the technology of World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.400 --> 00:45:15.120
II. It's brilliant, and it's brilliant partly&nbsp;
because Elon knows a lot about the hardware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:18.960
World War II. And so you think of World War II,&nbsp;
it's an engineering problem in the end. It's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:24.840
who can build better planes, better tanks, all&nbsp;
that stuff? But Carlin's doing terrific work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:30.180
And yeah, you're right. 1914 is a perfect&nbsp;
illustration because Princip, the assassin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:35.040
totally fails the first time. And how many&nbsp;
assassins get a second chance because the driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:39.960
of the target takes the wrong turning? That's —
Tim Ferriss: And then he stops right next to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:43.080
bar where you're getting hammered because you&nbsp;
failed your first attempt or whatever the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:52.380
is. It's crazy. Are there any other examples&nbsp;
of correspondence that you have been taken by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:58.800
not necessarily in the same way, but in a similar&nbsp;
way to the exchanges between the Rothschilds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:04.800
And the reason I ask is I consume a lot of&nbsp;
biographies, I do read a fair amount of history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:04.800 --> 00:46:11.400
but I don't really know how to assess&nbsp;
historians. And I wish I had a way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:16.200
establishing the biases up front because we're&nbsp;
all humans, subjective. There must be biases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:21.840
I'd imagine. So much like a conflict of interest&nbsp;
or special interest section in a scientific paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:27.720
like, "Okay. At least we know what chips these&nbsp;
people have on the board." So I have defaulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:36.420
to looking for correspondence that was never&nbsp;
intended for publication as a way to get a view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:42.900
into the thinking of the time, what was happening&nbsp;
at the time. Are there any other examples that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:50.340
pop up when I use the prompt correspondence?
Niall Ferguson: Absolutely. Part of what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:58.620
attractive about historical study is reading&nbsp;
the letters and diaries of the dead or very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:04.800
very old and the non-published material.&nbsp;
If you inhabit the world of the published,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:09.840
which many historians do, that is to say, they&nbsp;
essentially write their books on the basis of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:17.820
things that have been published, whether it's&nbsp;
books or articles, you are really doing research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.720 --> 00:47:23.640
at one remove from reality. I've written books&nbsp;
like that, and there is a role for them because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.640 --> 00:47:29.880
you are really synthesizing the scholarship of&nbsp;
many, many authors and trying to produce a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:31.440 --> 00:47:35.820
distilled version of what happened.
But there's a difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:40.800
writing a book based on published material and&nbsp;
writing a book based on unpublished material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:47.280
and it's a huge difference. I attach much more&nbsp;
importance to books that I've written based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:54.180
unpublished material and books that others have&nbsp;
written. Why? Because that which is published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.180 --> 00:48:00.600
is essentially filtered. A huge filter has&nbsp;
determined what sees the light of day in print.&nbsp;

00:48:00.600 --> 00:48:06.000
When you enter an archive, the only&nbsp;
filter is that nobody destroyed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.020 --> 00:48:12.060
and somebody thought to preserve it in&nbsp;
at least enough of an orderly form that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:18.600
you can look at it. And this is a radically&nbsp;
different world that you enter. It's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:18.600 --> 00:48:23.520
perfect representation of reality, but it's a lot&nbsp;
closer to reality than the published material.&nbsp;

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:28.380
And so let me illustrate the point. As I&nbsp;
write the biography of Henry Kissinger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:32.460
the first volume of which came out a few&nbsp;
years ago, I'm writing the second volume now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:39.060
there is, of course, a very well-established&nbsp;
narrative about the Vietnam War, about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:43.800
Cold War, about the opening to China, about the&nbsp;
Middle East. It's all very well established. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:52.800
are lots of books out there about it. But when you&nbsp;
enter the realm of the unpublished correspondence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.360 --> 00:49:00.480
including transcripts of telephone calls, even the&nbsp;
tapes of telephone calls, which were certainly not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:04.920
supposed to see the light of day because the&nbsp;
Nixon administration's the best documented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:10.860
administration of all time thanks to Nixon's&nbsp;
ultimately fatal error of taping everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:12.360 --> 00:49:16.860
the world is a very different one&nbsp;
from that world that you thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.860 --> 00:49:20.520
you understood, thanks to Christopher Hitchens.
I mean, Christopher Hitchens' book on Kissinger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:26.760
has sold many copies. It contains, I think from&nbsp;
memory, 12 footnotes. It refers to that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:26.760 --> 00:49:37.380
sources. It is a work of journalism of outrageous&nbsp;
superficiality. It's a polemical hatchet job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:44.160
and it shouldn't be treated as history. Hitch was&nbsp;
a friend of mine. I miss him. But it's important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:44.160 --> 00:49:48.540
to recognize that that's not a work of history,&nbsp;
really. Another way of thinking about this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.540 --> 00:49:55.800
what's the ratio of words read to words written?&nbsp;
Now I wish, Tim, that that ratio was published on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.800 --> 00:50:01.380
the front of books. So this is a one-to-one&nbsp;
book. He read basically one word for every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:07.500
word that he wrote. And this is a 100-to-1 book.&nbsp;
And this is a 1,000-to-1 book. And 1,000-to-1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:14.520
it seems like it's about right. I mean, most&nbsp;
books are really not worth reading because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:21.060
their ratio is close to unity. There may even be&nbsp;
books where the ratio's below unity, that they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.060 --> 00:50:24.347
written actually more words than they've read in&nbsp;
preparing to write the book. The best history —&nbsp;

00:50:24.347 --> 00:50:26.700
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like the&nbsp;
internet in a nutshell. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:32.220
Niall Ferguson: I mean, that's the blogosphere.&nbsp;
It's like, "Why bother doing research? I have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:41.760
great opinion." So I'm a very much 1,000-to-1 kind&nbsp;
of person. I like to dig deeply and broadly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:49.080
to enter that realm of the unpublished. And I'm&nbsp;
insatiable as I enter it. I can never be entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.080 --> 00:50:56.340
satisfied. I'm reconstructing Kissinger's life&nbsp;
almost on an hour-by-hour basis to try to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.340 --> 00:51:03.000
a new way of thinking about the 1970s, which&nbsp;
was a pivotal decade in a great many ways. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:09.360
rethink that decade is my current mission. And&nbsp;
of course, I won't succeed in changing that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.360 --> 00:51:14.760
people's minds because most people made up their&nbsp;
minds about Henry Kissinger long ago in about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:14.760 --> 00:51:22.380
I don't know, 1974. But I do think we have to&nbsp;
rethink the Cold War as a matter of urgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.380 --> 00:51:27.660
because we're in one. We're in a new cold war,&nbsp;
in Cold War II. And if we don't think long and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:27.660 --> 00:51:33.120
hard about what went right and what went wrong in&nbsp;
Cold War I, there is a high probability that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:38.460
are going to repeat some pretty serious mistakes.
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about Cold War II. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.460 --> 00:51:47.820
actually going to segue to your thinking and&nbsp;
observations of the not mutually exclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:54.660
combination of maybe conditions that have been&nbsp;
set over the last five to 10 or more years that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.660 --> 00:52:03.060
are now leading to certain types of inertia&nbsp;
in terms of trends, converging trends that may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:03.060 --> 00:52:07.620
or may not be avoidable. Then there's a lot of&nbsp;
wiggle room with independent actors and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.620 --> 00:52:14.160
as we've discussed. But I've already described&nbsp;
Cold War II, I believe, correct me if I'm wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.160 --> 00:52:22.560
as China, senior partner, Russia, junior&nbsp;
partner, in contrast to Cold War I. If you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:31.020
placing bets on likely events or&nbsp;
developments over the next handful of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:38.340
what would you put out as perhaps some reasonably&nbsp;
probable scenarios that we'll see develop?&nbsp;

00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:44.100
Niall Ferguson: Great question. The cliche&nbsp;
quote often ascribed to Mark Twain is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:47.460
history doesn't repeat itself, but&nbsp;
it rhymes. But he never said that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:52.380
Twain never used those words. That's a&nbsp;
made-up quote. What Twain actually said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:58.440
back in the 1870s was that that history was a&nbsp;
bit like a kaleidoscope. And as you turned it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:59.340 --> 00:53:04.200
certain patterns could repeat themselves.&nbsp;
So let's think about this as a kaleidoscope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.200 --> 00:53:07.929
You'll remember those things we had as&nbsp;
kids where you stick it to your eyes —&nbsp;

00:53:07.929 --> 00:53:09.360
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yes.
Niall Ferguson: —and it’s like, "That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.360 --> 00:53:14.100
psychedelic. What happens if I turn? Oh.&nbsp;
That's also psychedelic, but different."&nbsp;

00:53:14.100 --> 00:53:22.200
Cold War I had certain common features with Cold&nbsp;
War II, and there were certain very important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.200 --> 00:53:27.600
differences. So we shouldn't expect things to&nbsp;
play out exactly the same. On the other hand, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:33.000
clear that we have an ideological rivalry between&nbsp;
the United States and the People's Republic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:40.860
China. There is clearly a technological race going&nbsp;
on. There is also a geopolitical dimension to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:40.860 --> 00:53:48.180
conventional territorial dimension. And this is&nbsp;
a point Graham Allison made in his book, Destined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:48.180 --> 00:53:53.040
for War. There are some probability that they end&nbsp;
up in a hot war because what's a cold war? George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.040 --> 00:53:59.640
Orwell coined the phrase to mean a peace that&nbsp;
is no peace. It is something that has latent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:05.160
the possibility of an actual hot war. And I think&nbsp;
the United States and China have been in this kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:09.660
of cold war situation for at least four years,&nbsp;
maybe longer. I think the Chinese would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:13.860
say longer, but we didn't notice. We didn't&nbsp;
notice the beginning of Cold War I either.&nbsp;

00:54:14.940 --> 00:54:20.760
When Orwell talks about Cold War in 1945 and&nbsp;
Churchill in 1946 talked about an iron curtain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:26.280
most Americans were, "No. Stalin's&nbsp;
been our ally in World War II.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:33.360
You are just warmongering." And it took until&nbsp;
1950 when there was a hot war in Korea for most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:39.720
Americans to say, "Maybe they're right." I mean,&nbsp;
Kennan wasn't immediately hailed as a prophet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:45.900
when he talks about containment. The thing about&nbsp;
cold wars is that by and large, people in the West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:53.220
don't really notice their beginning, and take a&nbsp;
while to understand the scale of the challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.060 --> 00:54:59.580
So we're at that early cold war stage. I think&nbsp;
that's a good analogy. That works well. And also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.580 --> 00:55:06.180
this war in Ukraine is like the Korean&nbsp;
War. It's the first hot war of Cold War II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.180 --> 00:55:14.460
and it makes you get that you are in a cold&nbsp;
war. As in Cold War I, the hot war happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:14.460 --> 00:55:20.400
in a slightly unexpected location. The hot&nbsp;
war that kicked off Cold War I was Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:26.280
and the US hadn't really been paying very&nbsp;
close attention to Korea when that kicked off.&nbsp;

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:32.100
So if you take this kind of approach and&nbsp;
recognize that there are similarities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:33.060 --> 00:55:38.160
except that instead of a nuclear arms race,&nbsp;
we're in an AI race and a quantum computing race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:43.020
if you recognize the difference, which is&nbsp;
there's much more economic interpenetration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:43.020 --> 00:55:46.740
between the US and China than the ever&nbsp;
was between the US and the Soviet Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.880 --> 00:55:54.120
you are then in a position to start&nbsp;
hazarding guesses about what happens next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:54.840 --> 00:56:02.520
which is what you asked me to do. And these are&nbsp;
very difficult things to get right. And I often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:02.520 --> 00:56:07.320
think that if one makes a right prediction,&nbsp;
it's more luck that it turns out to be right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:15.480
than judgment because this is a complex system&nbsp;
of enormous complexity. So when we try to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:15.480 --> 00:56:22.380
anything about global politics, we're really&nbsp;
engaged in an exercise beyond our human brains.&nbsp;

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:29.520
But let me have a go. Right now, the US and China&nbsp;
are on a collision course over Taiwan. And Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:36.060
is to this cold war what Cuba was to the last cold&nbsp;
war. It's the flash point. It's an island in close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:36.060 --> 00:56:41.280
proximity to one of the players. Then, it was&nbsp;
close to the US. This time, it's close to China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.540 --> 00:56:50.100
It's an island which seems to be worth more than&nbsp;
its territorial size would suggest. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:50.100 --> 00:56:54.360
Taiwan's more important than Cuba ever&nbsp;
was because Taiwan is where 92 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.360 --> 00:56:59.880
of the most sophisticated semiconductors&nbsp;
in the world are manufactured by TSMC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:00.600 --> 00:57:06.240
And so in some ways, the potential for a crisis&nbsp;
over Taiwan is higher than the potential ever was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:13.500
for a crisis over Cuba. So I think what happens&nbsp;
next is that we end up with a showdown over Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:18.480
at some point in the next few years. Could be in&nbsp;
2024 when there's an election scheduled in Taiwan.&nbsp;

00:57:19.140 --> 00:57:22.560
And it would also make sense to do it&nbsp;
then from a Chinese point of view because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.560 --> 00:57:27.660
who knows who'll be president in 2025?&nbsp;
But probably not somebody as completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.980 --> 00:57:36.900
incompetent as Joe Biden. So my sense is that the&nbsp;
crisis is not far away even although the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:43.440
don't look terribly ready for full-blown invasion.&nbsp;
And that's the thing that I'll be watching most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:49.560
closely in the coming years. The other thing to&nbsp;
remember is, and this again is in the nature of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.560 --> 00:57:55.860
guess, that a characteristic feature of Cold War I&nbsp;
was the crises would happen in multiple locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:55.860 --> 00:58:00.900
at roughly the same time. So the Middle East&nbsp;
always mattered, and it was the place where things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.900 --> 00:58:06.060
would periodically blow up. I think the Middle&nbsp;
East is about to blow up again because the Iranian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.060 --> 00:58:11.100
regime clearly has an incentive to get into a&nbsp;
war because when it's at war, it can slaughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.100 --> 00:58:16.620
domestic opposition with much greater ease.
So I'm afraid that we are probably going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:16.620 --> 00:58:23.040
see a Middle Eastern crisis even before Taiwan&nbsp;
blows up. And what worries me the most, Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.040 --> 00:58:28.560
is that the lesson of Cold War I is that we only&nbsp;
narrowly avoided World War III, as I said already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.280 --> 00:58:33.720
So if we're in this kind of a scenario where&nbsp;
there are crises in Eastern Europe, potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:33.720 --> 00:58:39.420
in the Middle East, potentially in the Far East, I&nbsp;
think just keeping this a cold war will be quite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:39.420 --> 00:58:48.780
challenge. That will be a good outcome if it ends&nbsp;
the same way as Cold War I with peaceful victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:54.660
for the West. But there is no guarantee at this&nbsp;
point that Cold War II stays cold and there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.660 --> 00:58:59.700
guarantee at this point that the United States and&nbsp;
its allies win. And that's the real lesson of Cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:59.700 --> 00:59:04.500
War I. It was not inevitable that it stayed&nbsp;
cold and it was not inevitable that we won.&nbsp;

00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:12.180
Tim Ferriss: To follow up on a few things&nbsp;
that you mentioned, and as background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:13.920 --> 00:59:20.580
I was an East Asian studies major at Princeton,&nbsp;
spent part of '96, about six months studying at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:20.580 --> 00:59:27.120
two universities in Beijing, so I was able to&nbsp;
see the bicycles Beijing prior to the Audi and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:32.940
BMW Beijing that we see today. But the reason&nbsp;
I mentioned that is that I then subsequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:32.940 --> 00:59:38.520
traveled quite a lot in South America and a&nbsp;
fair amount in Africa. And I was able to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:46.320
quite obviously the extensive&nbsp;
infrastructure that was being built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.400 --> 00:59:53.520
by Chinese companies also seemingly&nbsp;
often with Chinese governmental support.&nbsp;

00:59:54.420 --> 01:00:01.320
And from a geopolitical natural resource&nbsp;
and resource distribution perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.520 --> 01:00:07.740
this is not my area of expertise, but it seems&nbsp;
like China has been playing three-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:07.740 --> 01:00:13.020
chess for a while now with respect to&nbsp;
all of that. As you mentioned, Taiwan is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:20.220
strategically from a technological manufacturing&nbsp;
perspective, non-trivial compared to, say, Cuba.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.960 --> 01:00:29.760
And my question is what can the US do? Or&nbsp;
is it a day late and a dollar short? I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:40.680
want to make it too fatalistic, but for the&nbsp;
US to be prepared or prepare itself and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:44.460
just be pseudo prepared, which we have been&nbsp;
for many things in the last several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.720 --> 01:00:50.700
what can be done? What would you suggest?
Niall Ferguson: Well, you are right to point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:50.700 --> 01:00:57.360
out that this is a global challenge that China's&nbsp;
focused not only on Taiwan or for that matter, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:57.360 --> 01:01:06.240
South China Sea on its own neighborhood. It has&nbsp;
been pursuing an ambitious and quite heterogeneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:15.720
global strategy since Xi Jinping came to par. And&nbsp;
the Belt and Road initiative is only part of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:15.720 --> 01:01:21.060
There are all kinds of other things, Digital Silk&nbsp;
Road, the export of surveillance technology to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:28.560
governments in the developing world, investment in&nbsp;
lots of technology companies in emerging markets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:29.220 --> 01:01:35.640
You can see the infrastructure when you go&nbsp;
to Africa. Even 10 years ago when I made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:42.300
documentary about this for British television, a&nbsp;
place like Zambia had really quite extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:42.300 --> 01:01:48.240
and visible Chinese presence, not only in&nbsp;
football stadiums and highways, but also in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:55.140
mining and agriculture. There's no&nbsp;
question that we confront a new kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:55.140 --> 01:02:00.600
of Chinese challenge because there really&nbsp;
hasn't been an era until now when China&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:00.600 --> 01:02:08.880
was so clearly pursuing a global strategy.
And by the way, that strategy was copied from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:08.880 --> 01:02:14.340
the West. There was a conscious decision by&nbsp;
Chinese leaders to learn from Western history&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.360 --> 01:02:21.300
and to try and adopt some of the things that&nbsp;
we had done that Chinese empires hadn't done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:21.300 --> 01:02:26.520
really hadn't even contemplated doing&nbsp;
since all the way back in the 15th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:27.600 --> 01:02:33.660
This hybrid China, which simultaneously has&nbsp;
continuities from Chinese imperial history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:38.820
from the Mao era, is also a China doing&nbsp;
things that China hasn't done in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:47.520
I think the United States has adopted recently&nbsp;
a strategy of technological containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:48.420 --> 01:02:54.480
that's most obvious in the recent restrictions&nbsp;
imposed by the Commerce Department that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:54.480 --> 01:03:00.300
designed to prevent China advancing&nbsp;
technologically by denying it access&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:04.740
to the most sophisticated semiconductors&nbsp;
and the equipment you need to make them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:09.193
Our strategy is to try to freeze China's&nbsp;
technological advance and we'll see how that goes.&nbsp;

01:03:09.193 --> 01:03:12.960
Tim Ferriss: Seems hard to do. Seems hard to do.
Niall Ferguson: Right. On the one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.960 --> 01:03:19.080
it's extremely hard to build TSMC in mainland&nbsp;
China, and I think there is some reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.080 --> 01:03:26.340
believe they can't. But the idea that you&nbsp;
can just use economic sanctions to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:33.720
your principal adversary permanently behind you&nbsp;
technologically to me historically seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:33.720 --> 01:03:43.200
a stretch and would seem also to me to raise the&nbsp;
incentives for Xi Jinping to take Taiwan. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:51.720
if he can't access the TSMC products, then he&nbsp;
can either try and get control of TSMC. But if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.720 --> 01:03:57.660
fails and we blow it up or they blow it up, then&nbsp;
he's no worse off. But we are a lot worse off. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:57.660 --> 01:04:02.400
think there are downside risks to our strategy of&nbsp;
technological containment that aren't being given&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:02.400 --> 01:04:08.460
enough attention. Broadly speaking, I think a&nbsp;
regime like China's is at its most dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:13.440
when it starts to feel that its strategic options&nbsp;
are narrowing and that there may in fact be only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:13.440 --> 01:04:20.460
a small window of opportunity to bid for primacy&nbsp;
or at least parity with the dominant superpower.&nbsp;

01:04:20.460 --> 01:04:27.900
We could do an awful lot more creative things&nbsp;
than we are doing in developing countries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:36.660
That is to say we could compete much better in&nbsp;
Africa. But the problem is when we go to Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:36.660 --> 01:04:42.480
we come bearing aid, it usually has lots of&nbsp;
strings attached to it. When the Chinese show up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.480 --> 01:04:49.440
there are not many strings and it's not aid,&nbsp;
it's investment, to put it very simply. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:49.440 --> 01:04:55.020
not really offering a particularly attractive&nbsp;
alternative. A concrete example of this is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:55.020 --> 01:05:00.480
we decided that it would be a bad idea for&nbsp;
Huawei to supply the world's 5G networks. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:00.480 --> 01:05:07.500
went around telling everybody Huawei very bad,&nbsp;
but we didn't have a better alternative. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:08.100 --> 01:05:12.600
one of my African friends complains that "You'd&nbsp;
come and tell us not to buy Huawei. We listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.600 --> 01:05:16.380
and then we say, 'So what should we buy?'&nbsp;
And it's like, 'Oh, we don't know.'"&nbsp;

01:05:16.380 --> 01:05:20.700
Or try Ericcson. It's way more expensive&nbsp;
and they won't give you any credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:20.700 --> 01:05:29.160
but try — that's stupid. We can't expect to win&nbsp;
Cold War II if we have no better product. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:29.160 --> 01:05:33.360
least when the Soviets were going around Africa&nbsp;
saying, "We'll give you the planned economy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:33.360 --> 01:05:38.640
we had a better idea. We definitely had a better&nbsp;
pitch than the planned economy. I think that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:48.660
what concerns me is that our strategy in Cold War&nbsp;
II at this point looks very risky and quite likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:54.960
to fail. And that's because when you read the&nbsp;
most recent national security strategy document,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:54.960 --> 01:06:00.720
we write things like, "We don't want to&nbsp;
have a cold war. This is not a cold war."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:01.920 --> 01:06:04.800
And then we go right ahead and list&nbsp;
all the things that we're doing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:04.800 --> 01:06:11.460
makes it very clear that we are in a cold war.
Tim Ferriss: That's the news that I see every day&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:11.460 --> 01:06:17.580
now, which is “so and so says we may be entering&nbsp;
a recession in six to 12 months.” I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:17.580 --> 01:06:19.667
am I seeing a different version of reality here?
Niall Ferguson: Yeah, I know.&nbsp;

01:06:19.667 --> 01:06:26.100
Tim Ferriss: Thou doth protest too much.
Niall Ferguson: The cognitive dissonance is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:26.100 --> 01:06:31.800
painful to manage whether you're talking about&nbsp;
the economy or about our foreign policy. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:35.220
that's what I'm thinking a lot about&nbsp;
and I do it tentatively because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:43.380
things could change direction in an unexpected&nbsp;
way. American politics is always a bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.380 --> 01:06:47.280
wild card, but it's pretty clear that&nbsp;
the pendulum is swinging to the right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:48.000 --> 01:06:52.320
That means that there's a pretty high&nbsp;
probability of a Republican president&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:53.160 --> 01:07:02.940
by January 2025. And who knows? If Donald Trump&nbsp;
is reelected, he could call off Cold War II in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:09.000
an afternoon. He could fly to Beijing and do&nbsp;
the best deal. "Such a great deal with my good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:09.000 --> 01:07:15.240
friend Xi Jinping." That could happen. And most&nbsp;
people struggle a little bit with that part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:15.240 --> 01:07:17.460
thinking about history because we like —
Tim Ferriss: Could you say a bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:17.460 --> 01:07:20.340
about that? Why would it be so easy?
Niall Ferguson: That's what he would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:20.340 --> 01:07:24.600
done if he'd been reelected. Trump doesn't want to&nbsp;
be in Cold War II. He thinks it's stupid. He just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:24.600 --> 01:07:29.640
wanted to do trade war. And what's funny about&nbsp;
the Trump administration was that Trump turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:34.620
everybody's attention to China, wins the argument,&nbsp;
convinces everybody China's a major threat, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:34.620 --> 01:07:39.420
all he really wanted to do was a trade war with&nbsp;
tariffs. And the point of the tariffs was to beat&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:39.420 --> 01:07:44.340
China up and then he was going to go to Beijing&nbsp;
and do a great deal. That was the plan. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:44.340 --> 01:07:50.400
going to be a photo op. And of course he didn't&nbsp;
win. And so that didn't happen. And ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.400 --> 01:07:55.980
the Biden administration has been more hawkish&nbsp;
on China than the Trump administration was. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.980 --> 01:08:01.920
that's also a product of American politics because&nbsp;
at some point in 2020, Biden's handlers said to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:08.340
him, "You can't win unless you are as hawkish&nbsp;
on China as Trump and maybe more hawkish."&nbsp;

01:08:08.340 --> 01:08:13.320
And so Biden, who'd in fact been anything but&nbsp;
hawkish during his time as vice president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:13.320 --> 01:08:19.020
on the campaign trail started to beat up on China.&nbsp;
And then they got stuck with this hawkish policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.020 --> 01:08:24.780
which is now dominating their whole strategy. I&nbsp;
think it's important to recognize that Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.780 --> 01:08:28.620
foreign policy, however you think about Trump,&nbsp;
however much you hate Trump, and many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:28.620 --> 01:08:34.200
listening to this will have major reasons for&nbsp;
hating Trump, but the foreign policy objectivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.200 --> 01:08:41.760
was quite successful and it was quite successful&nbsp;
without risking conflict. Trump was not somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:41.760 --> 01:08:48.060
who wanted actual war, he just wanted trade war.&nbsp;
And the trade war was always intended to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.060 --> 01:08:52.440
temporary measure. You were imposing the tariffs&nbsp;
and then you were going to go to China and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.440 --> 01:08:58.620
"Well, we'll take the tariffs off if you do this."&nbsp;
I can imagine a scenario where, quite easily can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:58.620 --> 01:09:04.020
imagine a scenario where Trump gets reelected and&nbsp;
does that. At that point, Cold War II would at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:11.520
least temporarily be in some kind of suspension.
Tim Ferriss: I'd love for you to poke holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:16.020
in a bunch of things I'm going to say&nbsp;
because I wouldn't call them terribly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:16.020 --> 01:09:24.600
well informed. We'll start there. Ample fodder&nbsp;
for conversation. The first thought is that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:25.320 --> 01:09:30.960
mainland China — well, first of all, I've just&nbsp;
been very impressed with the geopolitical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:38.460
both visible and invisible or less&nbsp;
visible strategies that have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:38.460 --> 01:09:45.360
deployed by the People's Republic. It's&nbsp;
always seemed very much long-term investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:46.320 --> 01:09:54.720
versus aid like you mentioned. And it seems to&nbsp;
me like if mainland China wanted to take Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.720 --> 01:09:58.380
there are many things that would&nbsp;
suggest they could do it nonviolently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:05.160
There are other ways for them to isolate Taiwan&nbsp;
just as an island. There are probably multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.160 --> 01:10:15.060
ways they could cut off various types of&nbsp;
access. It's not as easy for allies to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.900 --> 01:10:24.060
say, supply aid to Taiwan compared to&nbsp;
say Ukraine, where there's also imminent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:32.700
neighbor threat that is perceived in Europe.
That's the first thought to me is that it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:32.700 --> 01:10:36.780
seem like it would actually be that hard if they&nbsp;
were to approach it and say, "Hey, look guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:36.780 --> 01:10:41.220
nobody wants a violent outcome here. You're&nbsp;
going to be on your own. Maybe the US steps in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.220 --> 01:10:46.680
probably not. Your military, yes you have&nbsp;
mandatory service, but let's be real. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.680 --> 01:10:52.860
don't get a whole lot of practice." I have&nbsp;
to imagine that to your point though, moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:59.940
quickly could be to their benefit. And secondly,&nbsp;
and again, then you can rip all of this to shreds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:00.720 --> 01:11:10.020
Second is more an observation that perhaps even&nbsp;
if we are totally inept, we are still saved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:10.680 --> 01:11:19.980
in the sense that China and the US are so&nbsp;
economically entangled that China could end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:24.840
being the rider on the horse and the horse is the&nbsp;
US but they can't shoot their horse in the head.&nbsp;

01:11:24.840 --> 01:11:30.720
They still need the horse, whether that is&nbsp;
a byproduct of a lot of reserve currency in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.720 --> 01:11:35.460
the form of treasuries or US dollars or&nbsp;
whatever, or trying to sustain exports&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:35.460 --> 01:11:42.540
so their domestic economy doesn't face all&nbsp;
sorts of issues. The question then I guess is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:43.620 --> 01:11:51.060
do you see either of those holding any water or&nbsp;
do you think to take Taiwan it would escalate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:58.080
putting aside the Trump intervention,&nbsp;
to a physical hot war type of conflict?&nbsp;

01:11:58.080 --> 01:12:06.120
Niall Ferguson: The challenge for Beijing&nbsp;
is that the softy, softy approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.080 --> 01:12:13.320
which was essentially to use economics and&nbsp;
information warfare to bring Taiwan closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:13.320 --> 01:12:21.060
the PRC has failed. And it was obviously failing&nbsp;
back in 2020 when all Chinese efforts to influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:21.060 --> 01:12:25.860
the Taiwanese election came to nothing. And&nbsp;
if one looks at the sentiment of the Taiwanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:25.860 --> 01:12:31.260
population, it has gone not in the direction&nbsp;
of full independence, but it leans towards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:32.100 --> 01:12:38.820
we like the status quo and we'd ultimately&nbsp;
independence one day. The direction of travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:40.140 --> 01:12:46.740
from the vantage point of Beijing is very&nbsp;
uncongenial. That's the first point. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:52.740
second point is that there are things that&nbsp;
you can do short of an amphibious landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:53.760 --> 01:12:58.740
blockade being the obvious one. And&nbsp;
they showed us that they could do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:59.580 --> 01:13:05.820
but a blockade's an act of war. Even if&nbsp;
you haven't landed the troops on the beach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:15.300
you're still using coercion to assert control over&nbsp;
Taiwan. And this is where US policies is crucial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.320 --> 01:13:21.840
The US has had for half a century, more or less,&nbsp;
a policy of strategic ambiguity where it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:29.460
quite clear what we would do in such a scenario.
We acknowledged, this was one of Kissinger's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:29.460 --> 01:13:34.680
achievements, the One China principle. We&nbsp;
don't treat Taiwan as an independent country&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.680 --> 01:13:41.700
formally. On the other hand, we reserve the right&nbsp;
under 1979 legislation to prevent any violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:48.660
change in Taiwan's current status. The problem&nbsp;
is we really moved away from that ambiguity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.660 --> 01:13:58.560
the last couple of years. It began I think with&nbsp;
discussion in foreign policy circles of strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.560 --> 01:14:03.060
ambiguity. Do we really need it? Richard Haass&nbsp;
wrote a piece about that for Foreign Affairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.060 --> 01:14:09.420
but now Biden himself on three, is it four&nbsp;
occasions, has talked as if there's no ambiguity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:09.420 --> 01:14:15.780
We will definitely defend Taiwan. And although&nbsp;
those statements have been walked back, we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:15.780 --> 01:14:21.840
succeeded in signaling to the Chinese that we are&nbsp;
no longer committed to strategic ambiguity. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:22.500 --> 01:14:28.440
we also keep talking about practical ways&nbsp;
in which we will make Taiwan a porcupine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:29.100 --> 01:14:37.380
as able to defend itself as we made Ukraine.
We also, in addition to that, treat Taiwan de&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:37.380 --> 01:14:44.280
facto as an independent country when someone&nbsp;
like Nancy Pelosi goes there and every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.280 --> 01:14:47.880
aspect of her behavior is that which you&nbsp;
would expect the speaker of the House of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.880 --> 01:14:52.800
Representatives to engage in in an independent&nbsp;
country. I think from Beijing's point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.800 --> 01:15:00.780
we have shifted our position on Taiwan in a way&nbsp;
that is profoundly troubling to them because Xi&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:00.780 --> 01:15:05.820
Jinping's number one priority, the reason he&nbsp;
extended his time in office, let me be clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:05.820 --> 01:15:11.100
about that, the number one reason he did that was&nbsp;
to take Taiwan under the control of the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.100 --> 01:15:18.300
Communist Party. If we are saying actually, "No&nbsp;
ambiguity, Taiwan is an independent country and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.300 --> 01:15:26.700
we'll defend it," that is a major problem for Xi&nbsp;
Jinping. I think that's why even a blockade would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.240 --> 01:15:32.760
force the United States to take action. I&nbsp;
don't think there's a scenario in which this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:32.760 --> 01:15:40.020
administration would sit back and say, "Oh, well.&nbsp;
Never mind. That's just the One China policy."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:42.120 --> 01:15:48.060
Now my short term memory is failing me&nbsp;
because the second question you asked was —&nbsp;

01:15:48.060 --> 01:15:51.720
Tim Ferriss: There were two more or less,&nbsp;
let's see, there were two elements. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:51.720 --> 01:16:00.360
second element was the mutual life situation.
Niall Ferguson: Oh absolutely. Clearly this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:00.360 --> 01:16:06.060
something very different about Cold War II. The&nbsp;
economic ties between the United States and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.060 --> 01:16:13.740
Soviet Union were minimal and trivial, whereas&nbsp;
Chimerica still exists. Now this was one of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:13.740 --> 01:16:19.140
favorite Niall-ogisms. Back in 2007, I came up&nbsp;
with Chimerica to explain the neo fusion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:19.140 --> 01:16:26.100
Chinese and American economies, but I also used&nbsp;
that word to make a pun on the word "chimera."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:26.100 --> 01:16:32.160
The argument in 2007 was "This isn't sustainable,&nbsp;
folks." And sure enough it hasn't been. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:32.160 --> 01:16:39.660
there are still many people in the US who can't&nbsp;
get past the extent of US investment in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:39.660 --> 01:16:45.420
Chinese investment in the US, the trade deficit&nbsp;
is still enormous. We buy a lot of Chinese stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:46.140 --> 01:16:51.840
and they say, "And therefore we can't possibly&nbsp;
have a conflict." Sometimes this takes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.840 --> 01:16:57.060
form of "There won't be a war over Taiwan because&nbsp;
both sides would be so economically hurt by that"&nbsp;

01:16:57.060 --> 01:17:03.780
And I call this the theory of mutually assured&nbsp;
financial destruction. The problem with all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:03.780 --> 01:17:10.440
this is, to switch analogies, you could have&nbsp;
said all of these sorts of thing about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:16.320
United Kingdom and Germany on the eve of 1914. And&nbsp;
indeed somebody did. Norman Angell wrote a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:16.320 --> 01:17:20.220
book called The Great Illusion saying there's&nbsp;
no possibility that they have a war. It would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:20.220 --> 01:17:27.600
just be so financially ruinous. Well, actually&nbsp;
it's true that the UK and Germany were very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:27.600 --> 01:17:33.600
very interdependent economically in the early&nbsp;
1900s, but they stopped being economically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:33.600 --> 01:17:39.840
interdependent in a matter of days when war&nbsp;
broke out. Decoupling happened so fast that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:39.840 --> 01:17:46.860
it's amazing to look back on it, that the Royal&nbsp;
Navy literally cut the submarine telegraph cables&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:46.860 --> 01:17:53.520
from Germany to the rest of the world within&nbsp;
days of the outbreak of war. And legislation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:57.960
was introduced that effectively expropriated&nbsp;
German nationals' holdings of British assets.&nbsp;

01:17:57.960 --> 01:18:04.020
The US did the same when it joined the war&nbsp;
in 1917. I keep warning people that economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:04.020 --> 01:18:09.660
interdependence does not preclude conflict. And&nbsp;
you will be amazed how fast the decoupling happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:09.660 --> 01:18:13.920
when the conflict begins. And if you don't&nbsp;
believe me, let me introduce you to Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:13.920 --> 01:18:20.880
because what happened to Russia after February the&nbsp;
24th was unbelievably rapid decoupling from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:20.880 --> 01:18:24.840
western economy, not the world economy. They're&nbsp;
still able to trade with China and India, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:24.840 --> 01:18:32.040
they really got switched off very aggressively by&nbsp;
sanctions imposed immediately after the outbreak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:32.040 --> 01:18:38.760
of war. I think it's clear that our playbook is&nbsp;
very sanctions-reliant and the Chinese know this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.760 --> 01:18:45.420
and so they know that in the event of conflict&nbsp;
they will face aggressive American sanctions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.420 --> 01:18:50.760
And I dare say, going back to your point about&nbsp;
their ability to plan, that they're hard at work.&nbsp;

01:18:50.760 --> 01:18:56.400
In fact, I know they're hard at work right now&nbsp;
to try and sanctions-proof themselves and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:56.400 --> 01:19:02.700
to make sure that any measures like that blow&nbsp;
back on the US and cause significant harm to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.700 --> 01:19:07.740
US economic interests too. I don't rely at all on&nbsp;
the economic interdependence. I don't think that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:07.740 --> 01:19:12.360
any way an obstacle to conflict. It just makes&nbsp;
the conflict really expensive. But hey, World&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.360 --> 01:19:19.380
War I was really expensive. Didn't not happen.
Tim Ferriss: Is there anything then, going from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:20.340 --> 01:19:29.040
observer and a student of history to spotter of&nbsp;
patterns, is there anything that you are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:29.040 --> 01:19:36.480
right now or would like to do as an agent&nbsp;
of change to try to steer things in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.020 --> 01:19:40.620
direction or another? Doesn't have&nbsp;
to be specific to China. It could be.&nbsp;

01:19:40.620 --> 01:19:46.980
Niall Ferguson: Yeah, I would be strange if I&nbsp;
just sat in my study at the Hoover Institution&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.980 --> 01:19:55.140
having conversations like this and then going&nbsp;
and playing tennis. Part of what has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:57.360 --> 01:20:05.520
central to my life in the last decade has been&nbsp;
trying to apply history to contemporary problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:05.520 --> 01:20:13.920
and then do something about it. That means mostly&nbsp;
advice. I'm not probably that well suited to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:14.820 --> 01:20:22.200
political office. I don't have any aspirations&nbsp;
to be Henry Kissinger, i.e., go from being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:22.200 --> 01:20:27.000
professor to being national security advisor.&nbsp;
That's not really a plausible trajectory for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:27.960 --> 01:20:35.640
But I think that in advising people in decision&nbsp;
making positions, I can achieve something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:36.840 --> 01:20:41.460
There are a couple of aspects to this. One is&nbsp;
that about 10 years ago I created something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.460 --> 01:20:46.620
called Greenmantle, which I think you mentioned in&nbsp;
your nice introduction. Greenmantle is an advisory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.620 --> 01:20:50.760
business that works with a relatively small number&nbsp;
of mostly financial clients just to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:50.760 --> 01:20:59.460
the world using the application of history.
And it's been a way for me to do work that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:59.460 --> 01:21:05.220
is off the radar, not in the public&nbsp;
domain, that therefore isn't subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:05.220 --> 01:21:09.180
to the incentives that exist in the&nbsp;
public domain. When you do journalism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:10.320 --> 01:21:18.000
you can't be boring. You have an obligation not&nbsp;
only to inform but to, at some level, entertain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.840 --> 01:21:26.400
And therefore, the minute you enter the&nbsp;
public domain, you are going to make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.400 --> 01:21:30.480
more interesting than they may actually&nbsp;
be. Whereas if I'm working at Greenmantle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:30.480 --> 01:21:34.320
I just have to be right. And often&nbsp;
the right answer is quite boring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:34.980 --> 01:21:45.120
and boring but important is what I like. I'm&nbsp;
happily engaged in helping people who manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.120 --> 01:21:53.160
fairly large asset pools think about how the world&nbsp;
is going. And I can afford to be very boring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.160 --> 01:21:59.520
nerdy when I do that. I can be quite boring on&nbsp;
the subject of semiconductors or I can be quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:05.040
boring on the subject of the US treasury market&nbsp;
or monetary policy or whether the Chinese have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:05.040 --> 01:22:08.760
naval capacity to do an amphibious landing.
I spend a lot of my time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:09.840 --> 01:22:19.020
relishing the nuts and bolts, the granular&nbsp;
analysis that makes pattern recognition serious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:20.100 --> 01:22:27.120
Punditry on cable TV is the opposite of that.&nbsp;
And although I'll sometimes go on cable TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:27.120 --> 01:22:35.820
not least to sell my books, I hope I never engage&nbsp;
in the kind of punditry that is just designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.820 --> 01:22:43.020
get me retweets and social media followers.&nbsp;
You can always predict the end of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:43.020 --> 01:22:48.780
and get attention. My book Doom makes fun of the&nbsp;
pattern where the incentives to predict the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:48.780 --> 01:22:54.360
the world are very strong, and World War III and&nbsp;
the next financial crisis. You can predict that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:54.360 --> 01:23:00.300
every year and have an audience no matter how many&nbsp;
times you're wrong because people will always pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:00.300 --> 01:23:05.100
to be told the end of the world is nigh. I'm not&nbsp;
interested in that. I'm interested in doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:05.100 --> 01:23:10.440
kind of pattern recognition that gets it right.
Which I think, for example, Greenmantle did. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:10.440 --> 01:23:14.760
January 2020, we told clients "There is a&nbsp;
global pandemic coming. It is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:14.760 --> 01:23:18.480
hugely disruptive." And we got that right in&nbsp;
January, right at the beginning. Beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:19.200 --> 01:23:24.840
of 2021, "There's going to be an inflation mess.&nbsp;
They've really screwed this up." Beginning of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.840 --> 01:23:31.200
year, "Russia is going to invade Ukraine with 80&nbsp;
percent probability." That's part of what I do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:31.740 --> 01:23:38.520
I find it very intellectually rigorous because&nbsp;
unlike in academia, when you are wrong, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:38.520 --> 01:23:45.780
consequences and being wrong will get you fired&nbsp;
on Wall Street. That's very good for focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:45.780 --> 01:23:52.080
the mind. Second thing I do is talk to the people&nbsp;
who make policy or in the future may make policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:52.800 --> 01:23:58.980
And I'm always willing to go to Washington, or&nbsp;
for that matter, to London and share my thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:58.980 --> 01:24:04.620
with the people who have the very arduous&nbsp;
task of taking decisions under pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:04.620 --> 01:24:10.920
and under uncertainty with very little time.
I am privileged. I get paid to think and read.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:10.920 --> 01:24:16.560
Nobody in government has time to do either of&nbsp;
those things. You're just in meeting after meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:16.560 --> 01:24:22.080
and call after call. As Kissinger said, you draw&nbsp;
down your intellectual capital in government&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.080 --> 01:24:27.180
because you don't have time to accumulate any.&nbsp;
I'm always happy to put myself at the service&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:27.180 --> 01:24:32.400
of the people who have the difficult tasks. I'm&nbsp;
bipartisan about that. I would be as ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:32.400 --> 01:24:39.060
talk to Jake Sullivan as I was to talk to H.R.&nbsp;
McMaster. It's just that Jake never calls me up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:39.060 --> 01:24:46.680
but I'm here. And that's what I'm more than happy&nbsp;
to do with all humility because one thing's for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:46.680 --> 01:24:51.840
sure, the people in the situation room know&nbsp;
things that we don't know. They just know a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:51.840 --> 01:24:57.120
lot of things we don't know. And if you are in the&nbsp;
position of being an academic who pontificates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:57.120 --> 01:25:02.340
you should always be quite humble because&nbsp;
there's just no way that you know the half of it.&nbsp;

01:25:03.480 --> 01:25:11.040
Yeah, that's my approach. I think&nbsp;
it's something I got better at. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:11.040 --> 01:25:16.320
can certainly look back to the early days when I&nbsp;
was trying to be involved in policy discussions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.280 --> 01:25:23.520
shortly after moving to the US and I was pretty&nbsp;
amateurish then. I think the analytical framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:23.520 --> 01:25:29.160
of the book Colossus was correct, namely the&nbsp;
neo conservative project to build empires in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:29.160 --> 01:25:36.480
Iraq and Afghanistan will fail. And my criticisms&nbsp;
of the neo-conservative project were pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:36.480 --> 01:25:40.560
right. But I don't think I went about that&nbsp;
in a very intelligent way. I was too new.&nbsp;

01:25:40.560 --> 01:25:46.560
Tim Ferriss: What has changed? How has&nbsp;
the toolkit developed or the framework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:47.760 --> 01:25:54.180
how has that changed over time? What has made&nbsp;
you more professional and less amateur in how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:54.180 --> 01:25:58.380
you approach these discussions?
Niall Ferguson: Doing it with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:58.380 --> 01:26:09.120
team rather than being the solo tweed-clad&nbsp;
superhero. Ultimately, these things are too&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:09.660 --> 01:26:15.120
complicated and difficult for any one person to&nbsp;
be able to fit it all together and know enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:16.440 --> 01:26:20.700
When Greenmantle came into existence, my policy&nbsp;
was to hire my smartest students to come and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:20.700 --> 01:26:25.740
work with me to think about this and see if&nbsp;
applying history could even work. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:25.740 --> 01:26:31.920
crucially having taken them on to encourage them&nbsp;
to disagree with me and tell me that I'm wrong.&nbsp;

01:26:32.880 --> 01:26:41.640
And now most of what I will say on these subjects,&nbsp;
as in our discussion, is informed by a lot of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:41.640 --> 01:26:50.820
that other younger, smarter people do. I'll give&nbsp;
you an example. Back when the idea of Cold War II&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:50.820 --> 01:26:55.320
first hit me, it hit me because Greenmantle&nbsp;
was doing some work on Huawei and there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:55.320 --> 01:27:00.300
a map of the world which showed countries that&nbsp;
were using Huawei technology, countries that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:00.300 --> 01:27:04.080
essentially refused to use it, and the countries&nbsp;
that hadn't made up their minds. And I remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:04.080 --> 01:27:09.660
thinking when I saw the map, "Ah, it's a cold war&nbsp;
map. That's a recognizable cold war map, one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:09.660 --> 01:27:15.600
two sides non-aligned." And so I got excited and&nbsp;
started to think about this and I said, "Hey, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:15.600 --> 01:27:19.560
really need to work on that issue and that issue,&nbsp;
and what are we doing about challenges to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:19.560 --> 01:27:24.420
dollar? What are we doing about semiconductors?"&nbsp;
And out of that, those discussions came some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:24.420 --> 01:27:27.600
really good work by other people.
Chris Miller's new book, Chip War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:27.600 --> 01:27:32.760
which I highly recommend, is just the best&nbsp;
book on why semiconductors really matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:33.360 --> 01:27:40.860
And Eyck Freymann has done some excellent work on&nbsp;
climate aspects of Cold War II because climate's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.860 --> 01:27:47.100
cold war issue given that China's responsible for&nbsp;
a huge proportion of the increase in CO2 emissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:47.100 --> 01:27:53.760
over the last 20 years. So I'm glad to say that I&nbsp;
no longer fly solo. I work in a team. I've learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:53.760 --> 01:28:00.060
a lot from good leaders of teams how to build a&nbsp;
network that is greater than the sum of its parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:00.780 --> 01:28:08.100
And I think that's why the quality of analysis&nbsp;
is better than it was pre-Greenmantle when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:08.100 --> 01:28:12.780
was inclined to try and do everything myself. And&nbsp;
that way you make mistakes. I made some terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:12.780 --> 01:28:18.840
mistakes pre-Greenmantle. I got interest rates&nbsp;
really wrong in 2010 and Paul Krugman was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:18.840 --> 01:28:23.520
to jump up and down on me with hobnail boots&nbsp;
on because he was right and I was wrong and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:23.520 --> 01:28:30.540
I learned a valuable lesson then, if you're&nbsp;
going to go out there and do battle with Nobel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:30.540 --> 01:28:35.820
laureates, you better have done your homework.
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to take a left turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:35.820 --> 01:28:42.180
here. So we've been talking about a lot of macro&nbsp;
issues, but a lot of current geopolitical issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:43.140 --> 01:28:48.900
I want to ask you about fatherhood. So I have&nbsp;
a note here that fatherhood has been the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:48.900 --> 01:28:54.180
thing you've ever done. And as someone who does&nbsp;
not yet have children but someday hopes to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.620 --> 01:28:59.460
could you please expand on that? I'd&nbsp;
love to hear why that is the case.&nbsp;

01:28:59.460 --> 01:29:04.800
Niall Ferguson: Well I only have five children.
Tim Ferriss: You've got some work to do to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.800 --> 01:29:09.000
catch up with Elon, but you're doing all right.
Niall Ferguson: There are others who've beaten me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:09.000 --> 01:29:18.060
and I won't catch up. I'm too old to have any more&nbsp;
children because in the end you can have children&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:18.900 --> 01:29:24.840
at 58 but you will not be good at soccer&nbsp;
when they are ready for soccer and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:24.840 --> 01:29:28.080
have a moral obligation to be able&nbsp;
to go and kick that ball around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:29.700 --> 01:29:37.800
I have children who range in age from 28 down&nbsp;
to five and I can say unequivocally that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:37.800 --> 01:29:44.760
are the best thing that ever happened to me and&nbsp;
the thing I care, the people I care most about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:45.480 --> 01:29:53.640
and I never tire of watching them&nbsp;
grow, helping them in any way I can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:55.200 --> 01:30:03.600
Fatherhood for me has been more fulfilling than&nbsp;
I would ever have imagined. I think most people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:03.600 --> 01:30:10.860
most men, let me say, most men before they become&nbsp;
fathers, secretly dread the loss of freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:12.120 --> 01:30:16.620
the regime of diapers and the&nbsp;
prospect of being vomited on.&nbsp;

01:30:17.220 --> 01:30:22.140
And all of these things will happen when&nbsp;
you become a father. You will be vomited on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:22.140 --> 01:30:28.260
diapers will become a very large, and unpleasant&nbsp;
part of your life and you won't be able to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:29.880 --> 01:30:37.200
out anything like you were able to before.&nbsp;
On the other hand, there is something far&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:37.200 --> 01:30:48.600
more fulfilling about helping an infant learn&nbsp;
than anything else that's available to us. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:49.920 --> 01:30:58.440
I don't know that I've been at all a great father,&nbsp;
I think I've been okay. And the way I would think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:58.440 --> 01:31:05.520
about the role is it's very much pedagogical.&nbsp;
I very assiduously read to my children and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:05.520 --> 01:31:13.620
get them to read and make books a big part of&nbsp;
life. I think that's really critical because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:14.640 --> 01:31:21.240
these days as children grow up, there are way more&nbsp;
distractions than there were in my day and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:21.240 --> 01:31:27.840
have to make a concentrated effort as a parent to&nbsp;
make sure that they get into the habit of reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:27.840 --> 01:31:33.240
books because almost all the accumulated wisdom&nbsp;
of our species is in fact contained in books.&nbsp;

01:31:33.240 --> 01:31:39.540
And some of these books are quite old and&nbsp;
unlikely to be assigned in contemporary education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:39.540 --> 01:31:46.440
so that's a big part of it. Then there's all the&nbsp;
kind of that funny business of teaching children&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:46.440 --> 01:31:52.260
to be good. What is it to be good? What is it&nbsp;
to be kind? How does one actually convey that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:53.160 --> 01:31:59.940
How can one make a child creative without&nbsp;
over scheduling them with Monday's piano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:59.940 --> 01:32:07.500
Tuesday's coding, Wednesday's Mandarin,&nbsp;
that kind of typical parenting. And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:07.500 --> 01:32:11.700
still learning. I'm still trying to get&nbsp;
better at it while at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:11.700 --> 01:32:17.220
making sure that all the children got the same&nbsp;
kind of deal because I'm quite egalitarian about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.220 --> 01:32:21.780
these things and there can be no favoritism.&nbsp;
They all must think they're the favorite.&nbsp;

01:32:21.780 --> 01:32:26.160
So these are the things that if you gave&nbsp;
me the chance, I would talk about with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:26.760 --> 01:32:33.960
greater enthusiasm and at greater length than&nbsp;
then about anything else. But nobody really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:33.960 --> 01:32:38.400
wants to listen to somebody else talk about their&nbsp;
children, it's the world's most boring subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:38.400 --> 01:32:42.960
just like when people get out their phones&nbsp;
to show you pictures of their children and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:42.960 --> 01:32:46.500
then they can't find them because they have too&nbsp;
many pictures. A little part of you dies, right?&nbsp;

01:32:46.500 --> 01:32:52.560
Tim Ferriss: Well to me at the moment, this&nbsp;
is interesting. So since I have the mic in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:52.560 --> 01:32:58.980
front of me, I'll keep prying when you said&nbsp;
"I'm okay," and I might be getting the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:58.980 --> 01:33:05.160
phrasing wrong, "not great." Why do you say that?
Niall Ferguson: Absence. Because absence. Part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:05.160 --> 01:33:10.980
my life's journey took me away and continues&nbsp;
to take me away from my children quite a bit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:12.300 --> 01:33:18.360
There's that great moment in one of the Austin&nbsp;
Powers movies, daddy wasn't there. Remember that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:18.360 --> 01:33:22.680
We all love the Austin Powers in our family and&nbsp;
it's part of being a Ferguson that you watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.680 --> 01:33:26.880
Austin Power movies, you watch the Austin Powers&nbsp;
movies much earlier than is appropriate. But I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.880 --> 01:33:36.300
been away a lot. I mean, I was so negligent as&nbsp;
to take a job in the United States in 2002 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:36.300 --> 01:33:42.240
thereabouts when I had three children in school&nbsp;
in England and my then wife wouldn't move. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:42.240 --> 01:33:51.060
I remember saying, "Well, if I stay, if I don't&nbsp;
go, I'll regret it, so I'll commute." And that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:51.060 --> 01:33:56.280
a little bit crazy and I wouldn't advise anybody&nbsp;
to do that. It meant that I was away quite a lot.&nbsp;

01:33:57.360 --> 01:34:01.080
It probably made the breakdown&nbsp;
of the marriage inevitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:01.980 --> 01:34:09.300
So I think my older children would all&nbsp;
agree that they didn't see enough of me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:10.440 --> 01:34:19.020
I've had to compensate for those absences&nbsp;
by trying to be present in other ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:19.860 --> 01:34:25.980
so it's difficult. I think the truth is that&nbsp;
if you are trying to lead three careers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:25.980 --> 01:34:31.200
as you said at the beginning, if you're trying&nbsp;
to do good academic work and at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:32.100 --> 01:34:37.140
engage in some kind of public life&nbsp;
and then also do all this advising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:37.800 --> 01:34:41.160
sure you're going to work 17 hour&nbsp;
days and you'll be in New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:42.120 --> 01:34:49.740
and Washington and London and I used to travel to&nbsp;
the point of insanity prior to the pandemic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:49.740 --> 01:34:54.720
then the pandemic stopped that, forced me to stop&nbsp;
and for a year I lived in Montana and I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:54.720 --> 01:35:01.740
travel more than about five miles from the house.
And so I saw the two younger children every day&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:02.280 --> 01:35:08.460
and it was wonderful. It was wonderful. My now&nbsp;
10-year-old and I would go for a walk every day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:08.460 --> 01:35:12.900
We called it the philosophical walk and it was&nbsp;
after he'd done with his schoolwork and I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:12.900 --> 01:35:19.020
sort of done enough work on my then current book&nbsp;
and we would just have a walk and I wasn't doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.020 --> 01:35:27.360
that before and I'm doing it less now, so no, I'm&nbsp;
not going to get great. But the test is always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.140 --> 01:35:34.860
are your children going to turn to you in the&nbsp;
moment of crisis? Are they going to tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.860 --> 01:35:43.800
the good news when there is good news? Are you&nbsp;
able to remain in contact even when there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:43.800 --> 01:35:50.580
great distance geographically between you?&nbsp;
And on that basis, I think I get a B plus.&nbsp;

01:35:52.620 --> 01:35:58.860
And I would say to anybody who's highly&nbsp;
motivated, super-driven, type-A personality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:00.360 --> 01:36:04.980
do what you have to do, but remember&nbsp;
the relationship that you have with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:04.980 --> 01:36:10.440
your children is more important than anything&nbsp;
else. And on your tombstone, it doesn't say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:11.100 --> 01:36:17.220
“wrote 150 emails a day, or closed X&nbsp;
deals,” or it doesn't say “published 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:17.220 --> 01:36:21.360
books.” That's not what the gravestones say.&nbsp;
They talk about whether you were a good son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:21.360 --> 01:36:26.700
whether you were a good husband and whether&nbsp;
you were a good father. That's the gravestone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:27.480 --> 01:36:34.920
People forget that, but that's the gravestone.
Tim Ferriss: So mortality is as good a stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:34.920 --> 01:36:43.980
stone as any to the next question, which is also&nbsp;
related to parenting, and this may be a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:43.980 --> 01:36:48.660
comments that are locked in the amber that you&nbsp;
would revise. So I'd be curious to hear your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:48.660 --> 01:36:54.300
thoughts. So here goes, it seems that based on&nbsp;
some of the reading I've done several things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:54.300 --> 01:37:02.940
one is that you have been inoculated against&nbsp;
religious faith yourself on some level, but two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:02.940 --> 01:37:07.800
that you believe your children should understand&nbsp;
the Christian frameworks since, and I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:07.800 --> 01:37:12.480
quote you used, it is still the operating&nbsp;
system of North America and most of Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:13.500 --> 01:37:20.040
Would you mind saying more about that? Is&nbsp;
that a misquote? Did I find that on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:20.040 --> 01:37:25.560
Daily Beast? I don't think so. I don't think so.
Niall Ferguson: Most reliable source. Yeah. No, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:25.560 --> 01:37:28.620
was brought up an atheist. My&nbsp;
parents left the Church of Scotland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:30.660 --> 01:37:36.660
Presbyterian established church in Scotland,&nbsp;
mainly I think because of the sectarianism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:36.660 --> 01:37:43.080
was rife in Glasgow, the enmity between Protestant&nbsp;
and Catholics, which dominated the culture of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:43.080 --> 01:37:51.720
city in their day. And also because as scientists,&nbsp;
they embraced a kind of robust enlightenment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:51.720 --> 01:37:58.860
skepticism about religion and this was therefore&nbsp;
not a decision that I took. We just didn't go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:58.860 --> 01:38:06.240
church. And when we talked about those questions,&nbsp;
my mother would say, "Life is a cosmic accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:06.240 --> 01:38:11.804
deal with it." So that was how I was raised.
Tim Ferriss: High five, go out and play.&nbsp;

01:38:11.804 --> 01:38:16.740
Niall Ferguson: And in practice my parents&nbsp;
were still Presbyterians. We had all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:16.740 --> 01:38:22.740
kind of classic Calvinist behaviors,&nbsp;
work ethic, all that stuff, deferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:23.280 --> 01:38:29.100
consumption feeling of superiority, which I&nbsp;
think is inherent in being a member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:29.100 --> 01:38:36.300
elect. Just there was no God, no afterlife. And at&nbsp;
the point of death, your body began to decompose.&nbsp;

01:38:36.300 --> 01:38:38.340
Tim Ferriss: All the duty&nbsp;
without any of the payoff.&nbsp;

01:38:38.340 --> 01:38:48.240
Niall Ferguson: So ultimately it worked for me and&nbsp;
I don't think there's any kind of way I could find&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:48.240 --> 01:38:55.680
a religious faith, I would feel a phony. On the&nbsp;
other hand, I don't think it's a good operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:55.680 --> 01:39:01.440
system for a society and all attempts to build a&nbsp;
society on atheism have produced terrible horrors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:02.100 --> 01:39:10.500
I mean, think Soviet Union, anti-clericalism,&nbsp;
anti-Christianity doesn't have a great record.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:11.460 --> 01:39:17.880
So I came to the position partly under the&nbsp;
influence of Oxford, religious observance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:17.880 --> 01:39:21.720
as opposed to belief is very beautiful in&nbsp;
Oxford in Cambridge perhaps more beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:21.720 --> 01:39:26.640
than anywhere else. The choral tradition in&nbsp;
Anglicanism is one of my favorite things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:27.480 --> 01:39:33.780
And I think it offers not only some kind&nbsp;
of consolation, but it also offers social&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:34.740 --> 01:39:42.000
connections that you won't form in the workplace.&nbsp;
So I came around to believing that although I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:42.000 --> 01:39:46.620
didn't have religious faith, I should take my&nbsp;
children to church and I should make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:46.620 --> 01:39:52.620
they were educated in Christianity and indeed&nbsp;
in other belief systems, but that Christianity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:52.620 --> 01:40:01.320
would be the leading faith in our lives because&nbsp;
it's the leading faith in our civilization still.&nbsp;

01:40:01.860 --> 01:40:07.620
And it's silly to be ignorant of it. It&nbsp;
is the operating system. Tom Holland has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:07.620 --> 01:40:11.820
this terrific book, Dominion, which points&nbsp;
out that although we think we are secular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:12.600 --> 01:40:16.800
in reality, we are still a&nbsp;
highly Christian society. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:17.880 --> 01:40:24.840
I think that's dead right. So everything gets&nbsp;
colored by the assumptions of Christianity, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.840 --> 01:40:29.640
the discussions on issues like climate change,&nbsp;
which are supposed to be scientific in truth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:29.640 --> 01:40:36.720
fears of the apocalypse, the millenarian mindset&nbsp;
are deeply rooted in Christianity. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:37.440 --> 01:40:43.740
it's a slightly odd position, but not&nbsp;
without its precedent to be in a church&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:44.580 --> 01:40:56.040
without real faith, but with a kind of respect&nbsp;
for religious observance and a hope that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:56.640 --> 01:41:03.780
ethical legacy of Jesus Christ does not go&nbsp;
extinct, because I don't think you become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:03.780 --> 01:41:11.460
good person spontaneously as a result of Darwinian&nbsp;
natural selection. No, that's just not plausible.&nbsp;

01:41:11.460 --> 01:41:20.820
And so if you can use the Bible as a way of&nbsp;
helping your children find their way to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:20.820 --> 01:41:25.440
good, then I think that's to be encouraged,&nbsp;
even if you were as skeptical as I am about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.440 --> 01:41:28.667
the existence of God, the possibility&nbsp;
of an afterlife, all the rest of it.&nbsp;

01:41:28.667 --> 01:41:34.680
Tim Ferriss: So you mentioned earlier Hitch,&nbsp;
one of your friends, and as you described this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:35.640 --> 01:41:43.560
what I would love to know is did you explicitly&nbsp;
explain to your kids when you took them to church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:43.560 --> 01:41:48.780
what you were doing? In other words, there's&nbsp;
historical significance to this. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:48.780 --> 01:41:57.960
believe we're automatically shaped into good moral&nbsp;
human beings by evolutionary pressure, therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:59.100 --> 01:42:03.960
this is why I'm taking you to church. Or did&nbsp;
you simply expose them and run the risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:03.960 --> 01:42:13.380
them becoming die-hards and sort of engendering&nbsp;
a lifetime of headbutting with your children? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:13.380 --> 01:42:18.360
explicit or non explicit did you make things?
Niall Ferguson: I'm very explicit about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:18.360 --> 01:42:22.920
things like that. I think children&nbsp;
shouldn't be treated as children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:24.480 --> 01:42:30.540
which will sound slightly paradoxical, but it's&nbsp;
a good idea to treat your children as intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:32.100 --> 01:42:35.760
mini adults. And I'm sure Thomas, who's 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:36.600 --> 01:42:42.000
understood it once. I'm not sure Campbell knew&nbsp;
what I was talking about, but he's only five. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:42.840 --> 01:42:48.420
no, I think I'm clear about this and we talk about&nbsp;
it. Look, we have a very interesting religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:49.680 --> 01:42:55.740
heritage as a family. My wife Ayaan is ex-Muslim,&nbsp;
an apostate, who left the religion that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:55.740 --> 01:43:05.220
was brought up in having had a period of devote&nbsp;
adherence as a young girl. So we come at religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:06.720 --> 01:43:13.500
in a kind of unusual way. And so these things do&nbsp;
get discussed. And Thomas is a very articulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:13.500 --> 01:43:20.160
boy. So it would be impossible to take him&nbsp;
to a church without there being some initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:20.160 --> 01:43:25.950
discussion and explicit analysis of motive.
Tim Ferriss: So you mentioned Ayaan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:25.950 --> 01:43:32.520
so we might as well mention her again, Ayaan Hirsi&nbsp;
Ali, people should look this name up. How has your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:33.840 --> 01:43:40.800
time with Ayaan changed how you view&nbsp;
Western philosophy? Maybe even view&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:41.520 --> 01:43:47.460
the world or reality per se? I have to imagine,&nbsp;
I mean, just given her life's experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:47.460 --> 01:43:59.340
her trajectory that is so difficult for either&nbsp;
— I'll speak for myself, for me to envision in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:59.340 --> 01:44:07.020
any experiential way, how has your time with her&nbsp;
affected how you view things? I know that's a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:07.020 --> 01:44:11.940
broad question, but I'm going to leave it broad.
Niall Ferguson: Anybody who hasn't read&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:13.080 --> 01:44:20.400
one of my wife's books should do something about&nbsp;
that. Ayaan Hirsi Ali, Infidel is a pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:20.400 --> 01:44:26.220
place to start. That's her life story, her early&nbsp;
life story, which gives you a sense of what it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:26.220 --> 01:44:32.100
to grow up as a girl in Islam, in Somalia, to live&nbsp;
in Saudi Arabia, to live in Kenya, to experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:32.100 --> 01:44:37.260
radicalization, to be a member of the Muslim&nbsp;
Brotherhood, and then to go to the Netherlands&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:37.260 --> 01:44:45.540
to escape an arranged marriage, to gradually be&nbsp;
exposed to Western freedoms, to begin to question&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:47.580 --> 01:44:55.440
faith, eventually to lose that faith, to become&nbsp;
embroiled in the controversies that followed 9/11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:57.120 --> 01:45:02.880
to become a national figure in the Netherlands,&nbsp;
a member of parliament, then to be the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:02.880 --> 01:45:07.500
of a Fatwa, of death threats. All of this is&nbsp;
in there, and it's an extraordinary story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:08.700 --> 01:45:14.820
I've learned that the story there, which&nbsp;
is startling and memorable in many ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.360 --> 01:45:24.420
is the tip of an iceberg, a very deep trauma,&nbsp;
I think that's the only way to describe it.&nbsp;

01:45:24.420 --> 01:45:32.520
And we're still kind of learning about&nbsp;
that and learning how to deal with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:33.780 --> 01:45:41.460
So for somebody like me who led a happy life&nbsp;
in a loving family where I was given every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:41.460 --> 01:45:49.920
encouragement, it's humbling to think&nbsp;
of a life so different, so much harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:51.180 --> 01:45:56.160
and to realize that that's the life of millions&nbsp;
and millions of girls all over the world.&nbsp;

01:45:56.160 --> 01:46:00.720
So that's point one. Point two is, and&nbsp;
I dedicated the book Civilization to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:00.720 --> 01:46:08.520
Ayaan for this reason, we take for granted&nbsp;
the freedoms that we are given at birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:09.360 --> 01:46:15.300
and it's very hard not to take them for granted&nbsp;
if you grew up with the freedom of speech and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:15.300 --> 01:46:20.160
political freedom, the freedom to organize, the&nbsp;
freedom of association, all of those freedoms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:21.060 --> 01:46:29.400
But when you get to know and love somebody who&nbsp;
didn't have those things and had to be persuaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:29.400 --> 01:46:36.360
by experience of their value, then you see things&nbsp;
in a different light. And I learned more about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:36.360 --> 01:46:41.580
value of the enlightenment from my Ayaan than&nbsp;
I could have learned from any other source.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:42.420 --> 01:46:48.960
and that's partly why Civilization's dedicated to&nbsp;
her, because you've got to see the benefits of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:48.960 --> 01:46:57.900
free society with the eyes of somebody who's lived&nbsp;
under unfreedom, with all the discrimination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:57.900 --> 01:47:04.500
the handicaps of being a girl in a strictly&nbsp;
Muslim society, fully to appreciate them.&nbsp;

01:47:05.520 --> 01:47:13.440
Tim Ferriss: I'd second the recommendation on the&nbsp;
read. The story is really mean beyond remarkable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:13.440 --> 01:47:22.500
That's an understatement. And you mentioned the&nbsp;
fatwa. How do you both relate to safety and fear?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:23.460 --> 01:47:31.620
How do you think about how much weight to give to&nbsp;
fear of what could happen? And I ask, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:31.620 --> 01:47:39.300
in light of the not so distant attack on Salman&nbsp;
Rushdie as an example, right? It's not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.240 --> 01:47:47.460
fatwas are just forgotten. It would seem, I know&nbsp;
there's a lot of coverage around that and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:47.460 --> 01:47:55.440
different takes. But how do you think about&nbsp;
living life and how much weight to give to fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:55.440 --> 01:48:02.160
what could happen versus other factors?
Niall Ferguson: Well, I wouldn't speak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:02.160 --> 01:48:04.800
Ayaan. It would be untrue for me to claim that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:05.460 --> 01:48:16.680
doesn't feel fear. No, the attack on Salman&nbsp;
Rushdie was a heavy blow for us because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:16.680 --> 01:48:22.740
was a reminder that these death threats don't have&nbsp;
expiry dates. There's no statute of limitations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:24.600 --> 01:48:30.240
But fear, I'll answer on my&nbsp;
own behalf, is not a factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:32.280 --> 01:48:40.740
I don't fear these people. And I&nbsp;
credit that to a Scottish upbringing.&nbsp;

01:48:42.660 --> 01:48:48.120
My grandfathers faced far&nbsp;
greater peril in the world wars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:50.100 --> 01:48:56.700
My father's father served on the Western front&nbsp;
in one of the highland regiments as a teenager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:58.980 --> 01:49:04.920
And my mother's father was in Burma with&nbsp;
the Royal Air Force fighting the Japanese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:07.080 --> 01:49:15.120
They had reasons to feel fear. I don't, and&nbsp;
I have a complete contempt for terrorists&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.800 --> 01:49:25.500
that is not fake. I moved to the United States&nbsp;
after 9/11 because I was supposed to give us a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:25.500 --> 01:49:34.200
lecture at NYU on, I think it was 9/12. And of&nbsp;
course I never gave that lecture, couldn't fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:36.720 --> 01:49:43.320
but it kind of made my mind up to move to the US&nbsp;
and to take a job at NYU, which is what I did. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:43.320 --> 01:49:51.360
resigned from Oxford shortly after 9/11. I don't&nbsp;
think I thought it through quite at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:51.360 --> 01:49:56.640
but it was undoubtedly the Scottish impulse&nbsp;
to march towards the sound of gunfire.&nbsp;

01:49:57.780 --> 01:50:00.600
And ever since I've known Ayaan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:02.520 --> 01:50:08.820
I've felt a powerful obligation to protect&nbsp;
her and make sure that she is kept safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:10.080 --> 01:50:22.500
but never fear. Because historically, we face&nbsp;
many dangers. One can see that the world is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:22.500 --> 01:50:27.420
less dangerous than it was in many respects, and&nbsp;
in other respects, more dangerous than it's ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.420 --> 01:50:34.500
beaten. They didn't have to worry about nuclear&nbsp;
war. The risks of catastrophe are in some ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:34.500 --> 01:50:41.340
elevated by the advance of science and in other&nbsp;
ways, science is reducing our vulnerability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:41.340 --> 01:50:45.120
extending our lifespans, giving us a&nbsp;
shot, even if we're diagnosed with cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:46.320 --> 01:50:56.100
And I keep telling her and others the dangers that&nbsp;
we face include terrorists, but the probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.600 --> 01:51:02.040
is probably higher that we have&nbsp;
an automobile accident or one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:02.040 --> 01:51:08.760
of us gets diagnosed with cancer,&nbsp;
so be not afraid. And the culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:08.760 --> 01:51:14.340
which I love about my part of the world, the&nbsp;
culture of Scotland, is we don't feel fear.&nbsp;

01:51:15.660 --> 01:51:20.280
You can do anything to us, but you can actually&nbsp;
frighten us. And if you're brought up with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:22.020 --> 01:51:24.840
if that sort of really drummed into you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:27.120 --> 01:51:35.640
that's a great source of strength.&nbsp;
Of course, one fears odd things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:36.720 --> 01:51:42.420
I've always been more afraid of losing my&nbsp;
reputation or my honor than losing my life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:43.620 --> 01:51:47.940
But the things that the terrorists want to&nbsp;
induce, the kind of fear they want to induce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:47.940 --> 01:51:56.040
that is entirely foreign to me and I defy them and&nbsp;
I despise them. And I will use everything I can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:57.420 --> 01:52:07.680
to thwart them, because ultimately their threat&nbsp;
is a, it's a threat that you can thwart. Salman&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:07.680 --> 01:52:13.440
Rushdie made a terrible mistake in announcing that&nbsp;
he was dispensing with security and saying, at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:13.440 --> 01:52:17.880
time, he published his last book, "I need to live&nbsp;
my life." I said at the time, that was a mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:18.720 --> 01:52:24.120
and it was. That is not our&nbsp;
approach, but no fear, no way.&nbsp;

01:52:24.120 --> 01:52:30.240
Tim Ferriss: Thank you for answering that.&nbsp;
Niall, we've covered a lot of ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:30.960 --> 01:52:38.940
Is there anything else that you would like to&nbsp;
share, discuss? Any closing comments, anything at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:38.940 --> 01:52:44.520
all you'd like to request of people listening,&nbsp;
anything you'd like to point their attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:44.520 --> 01:52:53.700
to before we wind this round one to a close?
Niall Ferguson: Given that the risk of cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:53.700 --> 01:53:00.000
is not trivial when you're a 58 year old Scotsman,&nbsp;
I don't have anything else to say because if I say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:00.000 --> 01:53:07.740
anything else, I'll be late for my dermatological&nbsp;
appointment. So on the basis of rational&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:07.740 --> 01:53:12.300
assessment of risk, I really better shut up.
Tim Ferriss: I must go to my dermatologist, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:12.300 --> 01:53:18.840
makes perfect sense. Niall, and just for people&nbsp;
listening who don't get the visual spelling here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:18.840 --> 01:53:27.720
N-I-A-L-L on Twitter @Nfergus N-F-E-R-G-U-S. Thank&nbsp;
you so much for taking the time today. I learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:27.720 --> 01:53:32.100
a lot. I have copious notes, I have many things&nbsp;
to follow up on, and it's nice to see you again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:32.100 --> 01:53:35.160
So thank you for making the time.
Niall Ferguson: Thank you, Tim.&nbsp;

01:53:35.160 --> 01:53:39.780
Tim Ferriss: And for everybody listening, we&nbsp;
will link to everything that we discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:39.780 --> 01:53:45.960
all of the books, all of the resources, anything&nbsp;
and everything at tim.blog/podcast. Just search&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:45.960 --> 01:53:51.780
Ferguson or Niall, N-I-A-L-L, and it will&nbsp;
take you right there. Until next time, be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:51.780 --> 01:53:56.820
just a little bit kinder than is necessary. Take&nbsp;
care. Do not be afraid. And thanks for listening.

